{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1792620000000-engineer-8ab.prompt.md'","aggregated_output":"Created-Local: 2026-10-14 13:30 +07\n# Task: phase 8 slice 8ab, descriptors and BLAKE3 together (codex implements)\nWorktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p8ab`, branch `srvm-phase8ab` from `srvm-phase8` (= master 67b98907, phases 1-7 landed). Your work will be reviewed by a NON-codex reviewer (Fable/opus) with mutants. READ FIRST: `docs/agents/build-n-test.md`; `collab/phase8-plan.md` IN FULL (sections 1.2, 1.4 pinned constants, 1.6 SHA-256 sites that move to BLAKE3, 2 Q4 (the descriptor change is TWO rows), 3 slice 8a and 8b rows, merged as \"8ab\"); `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.derivations.md` 8.1-8.2 (regeneration, mechanical check) and 7.4-7.6; `docs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md`. Owner rulings that bind: A is the only code identity; R and H are derived checksums; BLAKE3 everywhere for the hashes that SURVIVE (dao.jing keeps SHA-256 as an explicit mintable registry member and its tests); the de Bruijn kernels (`debruijn/stack`, `debruijn/register`) are BACKENDS kept after phase 8, documented fragment, not widened; no version bump (ruling 2: the descriptor change is a regeneration, `lowering-contract-version` 2 and `contract-version` 4 stay); no compatibility shim; NEVER hand-edit a hash: every regenerated constant must be recomputable from a printed intermediate with the public function (derivations 8.1). \nDo (everything in the plan's 8a+8b rows, files and line numbers there, re-verify them): (1) remove the `:dim/lift-to` row from both descriptors (`debruijn_code.cljc` ~145, `debruijn_register_code.cljc` ~204); (2) move the H, R and descriptor hashes and the old de Bruijn dimension/node hashes that survive from SHA-256 to BLAKE3 with the explicit `{:algorithm :blake3}`/`jing/blake3` forms (`debruijn_code.cljc` ~138/548/564, `debruijn_register_code.cljc` ~200/258/269, `debruijn.cljc` ~139-140/966/1028) and the literal `:hash :sha256` descriptor rows to `:hash :blake3`; update docstrings; (3) move the descriptor-hash golden for H to a surviving namespace: `debruijn_code_test.cljc` ~364-368 (drop the two lift assertions; pin the descriptor hash, recomputed as `(jing/blake3 (dc/encode-scalar <printed descriptor>))`); the register golden `debruijn_register_code_test.cljc:7` and its duplicate in `debruijn_register_compile_test.cljc:446` (that file dies later: delete only the duplicated golden if the plan says so); (4) regenerate EVERY pinned H and R: `derive_test.cljc` (11), `linker_test.cljc` (2, helpers to `derive`), `debruijn_test.cljc` (6), `stack_parity_test.cljc` (6, producer to `derive`), plus any manifest/fixture golden that embeds an H or R (grep for the old hex values); verify that NO A address changes: `corpus.cljc` (32 pins) and `rows_test` (20) are gates (if any A moves, stop and explain); (5) amend `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.derivations.md` 8.1's mechanical check to \"differs by exactly the removed `:dim/lift-to` row and the `:hash` value\", and the `dao.jing.hash-registry.md` and call-site-classification text about SHA-256 H/R now being BLAKE3; (6) the `encode-scalar` hazard needs no move because `debruijn-code` survives: state in the report why. Write a small reproduction script `collab/1792620000000-8ab-regen.clj` that recomputes each regenerated constant from its printed intermediate (same method as the pins) and run it twice (byte-identical output). Portability: `.cljc` for JVM/Node/Dart; the hash functions go through `jing/blake3` on all hosts. If the plan and the code disagree, the code wins: report it, never silently change semantics.\nVerification, foreground, JVM only: `clj -M:kondo --lint <changed files>`; `clojure -M:test -e :slow -n <each touched ns>` and `-i :slow`; `bb test:sub:clj yin.vm` or `clojure -M:test -e :slow -r 'yin\\.vm\\.(debruijn|semantic-register|linker|ucf)\\..*'`; the whole `dao.jing.*` family (the lint sweep must stay green); root ids/pins of `rows_test`, `layout_test`, the harness must NOT move. Mutants (cp backup; diff and `git diff --quiet` to verify restore): one of the three H sites left on SHA-256; the register descriptor `:hash` row left `:sha256`; a pin regenerated with the wrong algorithm; a stale H golden kept. Which does the suite catch? Do not commit. Report `collab/1792620000000-engineer-8ab.report.md`: files and line counts, every regenerated constant (old prefix -> new prefix, how recomputed), the A-address gate result, mutants, deviations, design defects.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
