{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/design/datom.world.md'","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\nAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\nSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call. The one\nsanctioned function-shaped contact is a `dao.stream` handle operation, whose\nexhaustive outcome map is stream protocol, not an application callback.\n\n**Effects out.** Portable code appends an effect value describing what must\nhappen. A host interpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and\nappends the outcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by\nidentity in the data; neither side holds a reference to the other. A host with\nno implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported result. That is a\ncorrect outcome, not a gap to be filled.\n\n**Events in.** When the host initiates (a socket receives, a connection drops,\na timer fires) the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes\nnothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\nConstructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\nto data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.\n\n**Adapters.** An adapter is a map of those transform functions. That is all an\nadapter is. It is a value, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files,\ntimers and windows all deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is\nwhat they call. Each entry takes the host's arguments and returns one value;\nthe append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry cannot return a value\nto the host and cannot invoke application code. The adapter is handed only the\ndeposit operation and its own host resource. If its deposit channel fails, it\nmay close that resource to make the boundary observably gone; this is teardown,\nnot an upward callback.\n\nHost types are classified into plain data in the transform: it is the last\nplace holding a host error object, and no host type crosses onto the stream. A\ntransport may use a qualified envelope to preserve correlation, lifecycle, and\nadditive evolution; that envelope remains plain data and creates no separate\nevent mechanism. A composition may likewise define its own qualified vocabulary\nfor facts it owns.\n\nWhich stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the\nadapter's knowledge. An adapter cannot tell whether it deposits into one\ntransport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read. This keeps\nthe layering one-way: a boundary depends on nothing above it, because whoever\nconstructs the adapter supplies the stream.\n\nA transform deposits every event admitted by the boundary protocol and judges\nnone of them semantically. Wire framing, decoding, and protocol validation\nhappen below the transform: malformed wire input is transport machinery, not an\nevent. Once the transport has produced a valid host event, data is syntax\n(axiom 2): an event that is noise to one interpreter is signal to another that\ndoes not exist yet, and filtering at the depositing layer destroys perspectives\nnot yet taken. Retention is the medium's operational policy; meaning is\ninterpretation. Neither is the depositor's decision.\n\nTwo things are not adapters. One that exposes a function for portable code to\ncall isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the effect never\nappears as an emission. One that takes a function to invoke has relocated the\ncallback, not removed it, even when every value it passes is plain data; if\nthat callback has a meaningful return value, it is a synchronous call in both\ndirections wearing the shape of an event.\n\n## Architectural Commitments\n\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n  ordinary streams.\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n  auto-index observer.\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\nretired `:global` tag in\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n\n## Code Style\n\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n- Avoid cleverness.\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\n  leaves to review.\n\n### Problem Decomposition\n\nIf a problem appears complex:\n1. Decompose it into streams.\n2. Identify invariants.\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n4. Make causality explicit.\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\n\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; the orchestrator's append-only work log is [`docs/orchestrator-log.md`](../orchestrator-log.md).\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md collab/phase8-plan.md docs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md collab/phase8-plan.md docs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md'","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\n---\n\n# BUILD & TEST\n\n## Commands\n\n```sh\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \"name\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \"Remote test servers\" below for\n# which lanes may overlap.\n#\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` (G3) is the mandatory landing gate on master after merge.\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\n# overlap lanes, see \"Remote test servers\" below).\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\n\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\n```\n\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\n\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\n\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\n\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\n\n```sh\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\n```\n\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\n\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\n\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:python-antlr`)\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\n\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\n\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\n4. **G3 Master**: The mandatory landing gate on master after merge, and for any wide change (`bb test`).\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track (owner decision 2026-10-12; see \"Test tiers: quick and slow\" below). G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\n\n\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\nslice L5) is two tests:\n\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\n  REPL.\n\n```sh\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\n\n# ClojureDart\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n\n# Linting\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\n```\n\nThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\n[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\nnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\nDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\nwith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\n`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\nexits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\neach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\nscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\n(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\n`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\nunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\ndifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\npackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\n\n`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\ncompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\naggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\nstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\nbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\n\n## Test tiers: quick and slow\n\nThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\n(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\n\n- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\n  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\n  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\n- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\n  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\n- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\"When to tag a test slow\"): a deftest that\n  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\n  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\n  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\n  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\n  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\n  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\n  with the method below before changing the cut.\n- **Cadence:** the quick tier at every checkpoint (G1) and landing (G2, G3); the slow\n  tier before merging a big change and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\n  (the existing G4). There is no nightly run.\n\nMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \"Remote test\nservers\" for how to run them):\n\n| Run | Time |\n|---|---|\n| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\n| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\n| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\n| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\n\nThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\nSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\n`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\nunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\nA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\n\n**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\nrun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\notherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\n\n```sh\nNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\"test\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\n# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\n#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\n# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\n# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\n```\n\n**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\nshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\ntests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\nJVM test namespace under `test/`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-8}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/shards\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" | tee \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$OUT/npm.log\" 2>&1                   && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$OUT/build-node.log\" 2>&1     && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:python-antlr >\"$OUT/antlr.log\" 2>&1             && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nSUBS=\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\"\n: > \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\nfor s in $SUBS; do\n  bb test:sub:list \"$s\" 2>/dev/null | awk \"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\"\\\");print}\" > \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"\n  cat \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\" >> \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\n  echo \"$s $(wc -l < \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\")\" >> \"$OUT/plan.txt\"\ndone\n# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\nfind test -type f \\( -name \"*_test.clj\" -o -name \"*_test.cljc\" \\) | sed -e \"s#^test/##\" -e \"s#\\.cljc\\?\\$##\" -e \"s#/#.#g\" -e \"s#_#-#g\" | sort -u > \"$OUT/all.txt\"\nsort -u \"$OUT/covered.txt\" > \"$OUT/covered.sorted\"\ncomm -23 \"$OUT/all.txt\" \"$OUT/covered.sorted\" > \"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\"\necho \"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nrun_shard() {\n  s=$1; f=\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"; [ -s \"$f\" ] || return 0\n  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \"$n\"); done < \"$f\"\n  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \"${args[@]}\" >\"$OUT/$s.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$s.log\" | tail -1)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\nprintf \"%s\\n\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_shard {}\"\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-6}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nrun_ns() {\n  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\n  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \"$n\" > \"$OUT/$n.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$n.log\" | tail -1)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_ns; export OUT\necho \"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" > \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nxargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_ns {}\" < \"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\"\necho \"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\n\nTwo Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\nreached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\nand `git`.\n\n| Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (checked 2026-10-11) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Complete: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1-stable. All three lanes run. |\n| `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | java 21, clojure, node, zprint; babashka installed with `mise install babashka@latest`; flutter 3.41.9 is NOT the pinned version. Run the JVM and Node lanes here, not Dart. |\n\n**Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\nthat is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\nowner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\n\n### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\n\nRsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\nwould take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\ndirectories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\nbranch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\nthe host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\n\n```sh\n# once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\nssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\n  URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\n  git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\n  cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \"$URL\";\n  git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\n```\n\nTo test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\n`git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\nwas never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\n\n### Activate the toolchain in the right order\n\nA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\ntools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\ninto the clone:\n\n```sh\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"                       # puts the repo-pinned tools on PATH\n```\n\nActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-pinned tools\n(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \"Can't\nfind flutter on PATH\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\n\n### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\n\n\"One lane set at a time\" and \"one Dart lane at a time\" are per-machine rules about\nCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\n`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\"on one machine where\navoidable\"). On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\nlanes may overlap, with these limits:\n\n- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\n  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\n  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\n  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\n  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\n  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\n  by the design's own reasoning (\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\"), not yet\n  verified by a measured run.\n- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\n  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\n- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\n  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\n- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\n  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\n- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\n\nThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\nthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\nseparate Dart clone.\n\n### The full run\n\nRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\nserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\nthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\ntasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\nlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\nsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (omit the second on jupiter: no Dart)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\nDART=\"${2:-}\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nLOG=\"$1/target-ci\"; mkdir -p \"$LOG\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\" | tee \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$LOG/npm.log\" 2>&1               && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$LOG/build-node.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:python-antlr >\"$LOG/antlr.log\" 2>&1         && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"dart peer built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"  # shiva only\nlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \"$@\" >\"$LOG/$name.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n         echo \"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"; }\nlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\nlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\nif [ -n \"$DART\" ]; then                            # shiva only: its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\n  ( cd \"$DART\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\n    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\" 2>&1\n    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\nfi\nwait\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n```sh\nssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \"bash -lc \\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\"\"'\n```\n\nWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\npress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\nand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\nwrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\n`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\nscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\n(measured 2026-10-11).\n\n### Known differences from the laptop\n\n- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` fails on Linux.**\n  The assertion \"a bind on a taken port deposits bind-failed\" gets no `bind-failed`\n  event. A second UDP bind on a taken port evidently behaves differently on Linux\n  than on macOS (inferred, not confirmed). The test is in `dao.stream`, which the\n  semantic-register slices do not touch: treat it as a known Linux-only failure,\n  not a regression, until it is fixed or guarded.\n- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\n  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\n- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\n  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\n\n### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\n\nShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\nit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\nNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\nnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\nlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\nextra cores and a free laptop, and measure before relying on a speedup.\n\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\n\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\n\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\n\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\n\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\n\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\n\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\n\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\n   ```sh\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\n   ```\n\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\n\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\n   Common patterns:\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\n\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-14 04:30 +07\nAuthor: claude-fable-5-1 (Architect, plan only)\nBranch: srvm-phase8-plan at 627272d7 (= srvm-phase7)\nVocabulary: UCF §7.5.3 (cursor, cursor cell, cursor-ref, cell)\n\n# Phase 8 (cutover) plan\n\n## Summary for the owner (15 lines)\n\n1. Phase 8 is not one rename. It is four deletions (old semantic kernel, old v3 lowering/stack table, three old de Bruijn lowerers, old transport) plus one rename plus one hash-algorithm move, and they are coupled through 56 files that still build the old kernel.\n2. The rename is NOT drop-in: the old `yin.vm.semantic` exports `vm-load-program` (58 call sites) and `load-image` (11), which the new kernel does not have; the new kernel exports `load-ast`/`load-vector` with different argument shapes. Every one of the 56 requirers is edited by hand.\n3. Ten of those 56 are C4-track files under `test/yang/python/antlr/` (the other orchestrator's). The cutover cannot land without editing them. Owner must rule who edits them and when (Q1).\n4. The old transport (`handoff.cljc` 3783 lines, `checkpoint.cljc`, versions 0/1/2, 141 deftests) has no production requirer; only tests and two Python-track tests use it. It can die in one slice.\n5. The surviving de Bruijn code is larger than the design implies: `debruijn-code`, `debruijn-register-code`, `debruijn-register-effects`, `debruijn.cljc` (projection + `resolve-name`) and both kernels stay. Only `-resolve`, `-linearize`, `-register-compile` die.\n6. BLAKE3: 6 production sites move (two `descriptor-hash`, `image-hash`, `register-hash`, `dimension-hash`, `node-hash`); the descriptors also carry a literal `:hash :sha256` row, so the \"differs only by the removed `:dim/lift-to` row\" check in derivations §8.1 must be amended to allow that second row change (Q4).\n7. Pinned constants that move: 11 in `derive_test`, 1 descriptor hash in `debruijn_register_code_test`, 2 in `linker_test`, 6 in `debruijn_test`, 6 in `stack_parity_test`, 2 C4 manifest goldens, plus every pinned address in `corpus.cljc` (32) and `rows_test` (20) only if A changes (it must not; that is a gate).\n8. The descriptor-hash golden for H has NOT yet moved to a surviving namespace (`debruijn_code_test.cljc:368` still asserts only `string?`). That precondition of §10 item 8 is open and is slice 8a's first task.\n9. Fixtures from 7a/7b (`rows-checkpoint-v3.txt`, `ledger-v2.txt`) carry A addresses and no H/R and no format keyword; they do not regenerate unless A moves. `checkpoint-v1.txt`, `handoff-v2.txt`, `scalars-v1.txt` die with the old transport.\n10. Seven slices, 8a-8g. Estimated codex implementation 11-15 days plus 2-4 review rounds each; wall clock 4-5 weeks with two slices running concurrently where the dependency table allows.\n11. Irreversible order: regenerate goldens and move the oracle (8a) before deleting the old lowerers (8e); switch the production pipeline (8c) before deleting; delete the old kernel (8f) last, after the C4 suite is green over new images.\n12. Rollback is by branch: each slice is its own branch off `srvm-phase8`; nothing in `master` moves until the whole phase passes the slow tier and the C4 suite. Partial landing is refused.\n13. The biggest risk is coordination, not code: a mass rename of 56 files and 131 textual sites collides with any uncommitted C4 branch that touches `linked_harness.cljc` or the e2e tests.\n14. Documentation surface: 13 design docs cite `yin.vm.semantic-register` (127 occurrences), 25 files cite `yin.vm.semantic.md`, the old design doc itself (836 lines) needs a fate ruling (Q6).\n15. Verdict at the end: NOT READY, blocked on six owner questions (Q1-Q6); everything else is decided by the rulings already in force.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Inventory\n\nAll paths are relative to the worktree root. Line numbers are at 627272d7.\n\n### 1.1 Namespaces to delete\n\n| Namespace | File | Lines | Production requirers (src/) | Test requirers | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `yin.vm.semantic` (old kernel) | `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc` | 1058 | `yin.repl` (`repl.cljc:41`), `datomworld.demo.continuation-handoff` (`continuation_handoff.cljc:24`), `src/cljs/datomworld/demo/continuation_stream.cljs`, `src/cljd/yin/register_bench_cljd.cljd` | 52 files (list in §1.3) | delete; name taken by the new kernel |\n| `yin.vm.linearize` (v3 lowering, `lower-rows`, `lower-ast`, `rows-loader`, `ast-loader`) | `src/cljc/yin/vm/linearize.cljc` | 502 | `yin.repl` (`:28`, `:224`), `yin.vm.ledger` (`ledger.cljc:204` `lower-rows` in the derivation check), `yin.vm.linker.publish` (`publish.cljc:14, :159`), the two demos, the Dart bench | 47 files | delete; `ledger.cljc:194-204` is rewritten to check `sr-linearize/project-rows` |\n| `yin.vm.code` (v3 stack table) | `src/cljc/yin/vm/code.cljc` | 467 | `linker.cljc:45` (28 `code/` call sites, mostly `semantic-format` scanners), `ledger.cljc` (`code/well-formed-vector?` `:194`), `completion.cljc:206, :256` (`project-segment-qualified`), `semantic.cljc`, `ucf.cljc:32` (require only, docstring use), `publish.cljc:8` (`materialize-vector!`), `handoff.cljc:57`, `debruijn_code.cljc`, `debruijn_linearize.cljc` | 10 files | delete the table and `semantic-format`; `completion.cljc`'s two uses need a `sr-code` equivalent or the completion namespace moves to the v4 table (see Q5). `materialize-vector!` moves to `sr-code` or `yin.vm` |\n| `yin.vm.debruijn-resolve` | `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_resolve.cljc` | 624 | `linker.cljc:52` (`relowered-hashes` `:2460`), the two other old lowerers | `test_utils.cljc:24` (`resolved-of` `:200-210`), `debruijn_resolve_test`, `debruijn_register_contract_test`, `debruijn_register_benchmark_test`, `derive_test` | delete after 8a |\n| `yin.vm.debruijn-linearize` | `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_linearize.cljc` | 422 | `repl.cljc:28, :232`, `linker.cljc:49, :2171, :2462`, `publish.cljc:10, :161` | 37 files (every test that builds a stack kernel through `dl/adapt`; the 10 C4 files among them) | delete after 8c |\n| `yin.vm.debruijn-register-compile` | `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_compile.cljc` | 840 | `repl.cljc:30, :237`, `linker.cljc:51, :2175, :2464`, `publish.cljc:12, :162` | 36 files | delete after 8c |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.handoff` (old transport, versions 0/1/2, `export-task` `:1392`, `resume-task` `:3778`, `codec-versions` `:3253`) | `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/handoff.cljc` | 3783 | none | 15 files: `handoff_test`, `handoff_v1_test`, `handoff_v2_{authority,census,custody,gates,holder,layout,test,validity,values,walker}`, `checkpoint_test`, `lift_v1_test`, `scalar_round_trip_test`, `ucf/safepoint_harness_test`, `lift_support`, `v2_support`; C4: `int_heap_test.cljc:241` (`export-task`), `int_contract_test.cljc:483` (`resume-task`) | delete (Q2) |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.checkpoint` (old inspector, `inspect` `:510`, `v2-version` `:58`) | `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/checkpoint.cljc` | 541 | `handoff.cljc:69` only | `checkpoint_test`, `lift_v1_test`, `handoff_v2_authority_test` | delete with handoff; `yin.vm.ucf.rows.checkpoint` is the surviving inspector (required by holder, authority, export) |\n\nTest-support namespaces that die with them: `test/yin/vm/ucf/lift_support.cljc` (454 lines; requires old `semantic`, `linearize`, `handoff`), `test/yin/vm/ucf/v2_support.cljc` (569 lines; requires all of the above plus `dl`/`rc`), `test/yin/vm/ucf/checkpoint_fixtures.cljc` (reads `checkpoint-v1.txt`). **Hazard:** three NEW tests borrow from them: `semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test`, `semantic_register/frame_test` (both require `lift-support` and `v2-support`), `semantic_register/layout_test` (`v2-support`), and `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_support.cljc` requires `lift-support`. Slice 8d must first move the borrowed helpers (stream/ring construction, `new-machine`, program fixtures) into `rows_support.cljc` or a new `yin.vm.semantic-register.support`, then delete.\n\nFixtures that die: `test/resources/yin/vm/ucf/checkpoint-v1.txt` (524 hashes), `handoff-v2.txt` (163), `scalars-v1.txt` (96) and its minter `scalars-v1.mint.clj`. Fixtures that stay: `rows-checkpoint-v3.txt` (838 hashes, A addresses only, written 7a/7b), `ledger-v2.txt` (21). Neither contains the format keyword or an H/R (checked by hex grep for `semantic-register` and `semantic/code`: 0 hits in all five files).\n\n### 1.2 Namespaces that survive (contrary to a naive reading of \"delete the de Bruijn code\")\n\n| Namespace | Why it stays | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| `yin.vm.debruijn-code` (`image-hash`, `encode-scalar`, `encode-image`, descriptor) | computes H; kernels, layout, derive, handoff-replacement all require it | required by `debruijn/stack.cljc`, `semantic_register/layout.cljc:35`, `semantic_register/derive.cljc`, `linker.cljc:48`, `publish.cljc:9`, `repl.cljc:27` |\n| `yin.vm.debruijn-register-code` (`register-hash`, descriptor) | computes R | `debruijn/register.cljc`, `layout.cljc:36`, `derive.cljc`, `linker.cljc:50`, `publish.cljc:11` |\n| `yin.vm.debruijn-register-effects` | kernel dependency | `debruijn/register.cljc`, `handoff.cljc:62` |\n| `yin.vm.debruijn` (1641 lines; projection D5 and `resolve-name` `:617`) | `pipeline.cljc:39` uses `project-datoms`, `projected->datoms`, `frame-datoms`, `exception-diagnostic`; `derive.cljc:33` and `scan.cljc:37` use `resolve-name` | survives whole; its `dimension-hash` (`:957`) and `node-hash` (`:1020`) are SHA-256 and MOVE to BLAKE3 (owner: surviving hashes move) |\n| `yin.vm.debruijn.stack`, `yin.vm.debruijn.register` | backends, owner ruling 2026-10-13 | 45 and 39 requirers |\n\nThe `encode-scalar` hazard: `debruijn_register_code.cljc:231, :234, :245, :258` call `debruijn-code/encode-scalar`, and its descriptor names it as data (`:201` `:scalar-encoder :yin.debruijn-code/encode-scalar`). Because `debruijn-code` survives, the hazard is not a dangling reference; it is that **`encode-scalar` is tested only in `debruijn_code_test.cljc` (`:120-276`), which also tests the `:dim/lift-to` row (`:364-367`)**. Slice 8a edits that test in place (drop the two lift-to assertions, pin the descriptor hash) and must not delete it.\n\n### 1.3 Namespace to rename: `yin.vm.semantic-register` to `yin.vm.semantic`\n\nFiles: `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic_register.cljc` (490 lines) plus `semantic_register/{analysis,code,derive,frame,layout,linearize,scan}.cljc`. Tests: `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/` (16 files) move to `test/yin/vm/semantic/`.\n\nCounts (grep at 627272d7):\n\n| Where | textual `yin.vm.semantic-register*` occurrences | files |\n|---|---|---|\n| src/ + test/ | 131 | 33 |\n| docs/ | 127 | 13 (`universal-continuation-format.md` 20, `continuation.md` 24, `evaluator.md` 21, `derivations.md` 17, `ucf-transport-tuples.md` 10, `semantic-register-vm.md` 8, `code-as-tuples.md` 6, `linker.md` 4, `orchestrator-log.md` 4, `cursor-cell-numbering.md` 3, `debruijn.stack.md` 1, `debruijn.register.md` 1, `debruijn.targets.md` 1) |\n| keyword `:yin.semantic-register/code` | 11 | `semantic_register.cljc:410, :446, :456`, `linker.cljc:719`, `rows.cljc:2118`, `repl/serve_test.cljc:450`, `linker_test.cljc:564`, `semantic_register/link_test.cljc:80, :127, :128, :157` |\n| alias `:as sr` | 11 | tests |\n\nOld-name requirers that must be rewritten to the new API (56 files; `semantic/create-vm` 92 sites, `semantic/vm-load-program` 58, `semantic/load-vector` 44, `semantic/load-image` 11, `semantic/attach-image` 5, `semantic/semantic-restore` 2):\n\n- src (4): `yin/repl.cljc`, `datomworld/demo/continuation_handoff.cljc`, `src/cljs/datomworld/demo/continuation_stream.cljs`, `src/cljd/yin/register_bench_cljd.cljd`.\n- yin.vm tests (34): `vm_test`, `attach_image_test`, `cell_test`, `completion_test`, `continuation_invoke_test`, `data_test`, `debruijn/register_test`, `debruijn/stack_effects_test`, `debruijn/stack_parity_test`, `effect_forgery_test`, `engine_gate_test`, `ffi_test`, `ffi/remote_serve/responder_test`, `heap_reclamation_test`, `host_typed_values_test`, `integer_test`, `linearize_test` (dies), `linker_manifest_test`, `linker_require_test`, `linker_step_test`, `linker_test`, `linker/dht_test`, `linker/publish_test`, `linker/remote_transfer_test`, `mint_test`, `rule_r_test`, `semantic_engine_test`, `semantic_ffi_test`, `semantic_stream_observer_test`, `semantic_test`, `stream_poll_test`, `telemetry_test`, `test_utils` (`:168` `rows-loader semantic/load-vector`), `ucf_test`, `ucf/handoff_test` (dies), `ucf/handoff_v1_test` (dies), `ucf/lift_support` (dies), `ucf/remote_test`, `ucf/v2_support` (dies).\n- other (3): `bench/yin_vm_bench`, `datomworld/demo/continuation_handoff_test`, `yin/repl/dht_process_test.clj`.\n- **C4 track (10, owner question Q1):** `yang/python/antlr/{e2e_c2_test.clj, e2e_test.clj, float_address_test, int_contract_test, int_heap_test, int_literal_test, int_ops_test, linked_harness, prelude_parity_test, safepoint_test}` and `linked_harness.cljc:105-120` which binds `:semantic` to `linearize/ast-loader semantic/vm-load-program` and `:stack`/`:register` to `dl/adapt`/`rc/adapt`.\n\nAPI mapping the rewrite uses (new names at `semantic_register.cljc`): `create-vm` `:350` (same option map), `load-vector` `:303` (`[machine v stamp]`, v4 vector), `attach-image` `:314`, `load-ast` `:322` (`[machine datoms stamp]`, projects through `sr-linearize/project-datoms`). There is no `vm-load-program` (the old datom-batch loader) and no `load-image`; `semantic-restore` is `register-restore` `:101`. So the 58 `vm-load-program` sites become `load-ast` over `vm/ast->datoms`, or `load-vector` over `(:vector (sr-linearize/project ast))`.\n\nMechanics (recommendation in §2, Q3): `git mv` the eight source files and 16 test files in one commit with no content change except the `ns` forms and require sites, so the rename is reviewable as pure moves; the API rewrite of the 56 requirers is a separate commit in the same slice.\n\n### 1.4 Every pinned H, R, descriptor hash and A address\n\n| Constant | File:line | What it pins | Phase-8 action |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 H + 1 R pair | `semantic_register/derive_test.cljc:240` | §6.3 row 6 image hashes of the new lowerer | regenerate (descriptor + BLAKE3) |\n| 10 R hashes | `derive_test.cljc:419-428` | `register-goldens` | regenerate |\n| 1 R descriptor hash `7eacba41…` | `debruijn_register_code_test.cljc:7` (already moved at 5b; duplicate at `debruijn_register_compile_test.cljc:446` dies) | `rcode/descriptor-hash` | regenerate |\n| H descriptor hash | `debruijn_code_test.cljc:368` is still `(is (string? dc/descriptor-hash))` | NOT pinned | **pin at 8a before anything else** (§10 item 8 precondition) |\n| H `52791d4a…`, R `c0aefe2f…` | `linker_test.cljc:522-531` (`pinned-identities-are-host-independent`), helpers `stack-image`/`register-image` `:253-260` use `dl/adapt`/`rc/adapt` | the one host-independence golden | helpers switch to `derive`, both strings regenerate |\n| 12 R hashes/images | `debruijn_register_compile_test.cljc:449-620` | old lowerer | deleted, not regenerated (§8.1) |\n| 6 node/dimension hashes | `debruijn_test.cljc:136, :933, :1300-1306` | projection `dimension-hash`, node hashes (SHA-256) | regenerate under BLAKE3 (surviving hashes move) |\n| 6 image hashes + 2 hex images | `debruijn/stack_parity_test.cljc:321-347` | old `lower-stack` output and H of corpus rows (incl. `52791d4a…`) | the images are the old lowerer's; either regenerate from `derive/stack-image` (if bytes equal) or delete the byte pins and keep behavioural parity (recommend: regenerate, and the test's producer becomes `derive`) |\n| `manifest-golden` `b9dbaeff…` | `yang/python/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc:223-235` | py manifest address (JVM golden) | regenerate (C4 file, Q1) |\n| `pysp-manifest-golden` `0971cc1e…` | `linked_safepoint_test.cljc:240-247` | pysp manifest | regenerate (C4 file, Q1) |\n| 5 + 1 segment addresses | `int_literal_test.cljc` (5), `float_address_test.cljc:227-269` (1 + 4 fragments) | A addresses of Python literals under the OLD v3 vector? To verify at 8f: if these are tree or v4 addresses they hold; if v3 vector addresses they die with v3 | verify, regenerate only if they are v3 (C4 files, Q1) |\n| 32 A addresses | `semantic_register/corpus.cljc` | v4 vector addresses | must NOT change; a gate, not a regeneration |\n| 20 addresses | `ucf/rows_test.cljc` | pack roots / A | must NOT change |\n| 2 | `yang/safepoint_test.cljc` | to verify | as above |\n| scanner goldens | `semantic_register/scanner_goldens.cljc` (7231 lines, v3 answers at 993d5411) | historical evidence | stays; the live comparisons in `scan_test.cljc:65-67, :188, :277-300` against `old-linearize`/`old-code` are deleted, `footprint-mnemonics-is-the-v3-table-minus-push` `:261-264` (`code/mnemonics`) is rewritten to compare against the pinned v3 mnemonic set as data |\n| manifests' `:yin.module/index` | `publish.cljc:189` `{h h-address r r-address}` | | new shape `{:yin.debruijn.code h-addr :yin.debruijn.register r-addr}` (derivations §8.1) |\n\nFixtures that would regenerate only if A moves: `rows-checkpoint-v3.txt`, `ledger-v2.txt` (both 7a/7b). Gate: `(= (:vector (sr-linearize/project ast)) <before>)` is unaffected by phase 8 because the v4 vector, `sr-code/contract \"v4\"` and `jing/segment-key` are untouched; the rename changes no hashed content (the keyword `:yin.semantic-register/code` is not in any pack: hex grep 0 hits).\n\n### 1.5 The old transport paths and who exercises them\n\n| Path | Location | Exercised by | Phase 8 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| version 0/1/2 codec, `codec-versions` | `handoff.cljc:3253-3310`, `v2-body?` `:1993` | `handoff_test` (24), `handoff_v1_test` (27), `handoff_v2_*` (44 across 10 files), `lift_v1_test` (21), `scalar_round_trip_test` (2) | delete (Q2) |\n| `export-task`/`resume-task` | `handoff.cljc:1392, :3778` | the above + `int_heap_test.cljc:241`, `int_contract_test.cljc:483` (C4) | delete; the two C4 calls move to the holder path (`yin.vm.ucf.holder.export`/`driver`) |\n| old inspector `checkpoint/inspect` | `checkpoint.cljc:510`, `inspect-body` `:476` | `checkpoint_test` (15), `handoff_v2_authority_test`, `lift_v1_test` | delete; `rows/checkpoint.cljc` is the inspector |\n| old harness | `test/yin/vm/ucf/safepoint_harness_test.cljc` (8 deftests, over `handoff`) | | delete; `semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc` (26 deftests) is the harness |\n| `ucf/contract-stamp` `{\"v3\", version 0}` and `ucf/profiles` (version-2 registry) | `ucf.cljc:47-68` | `ucf_test.cljc:164-354` (14 sites over `vm/semantic-contract`) | `contract-stamp` becomes the rows profile (`rows.cljc:36`), `profiles` deleted with version 2; `ucf_test` rewritten to v4 vectors |\n| `vm/semantic-contract \"v3\"` | `vm.cljc:266-269`; users `linker.cljc:706`, `ledger.cljc:49`, `completion.cljc:714`, `ucf.cljc:47, :59, :266`, `publish.cljc:174`, `scan.cljc:76` (footprint-table key), `vm.cljc:1755` (footprint table) | | becomes `\"v4\"` (= `sr-code/contract`); the footprint table is re-keyed; the manifest contracts map moves `\"v3\"` to `\"v4\"` (§8.1) |\n\nDeftest counts at stake: old-kernel direct tests 91 (`semantic_test`, `semantic_engine_test`, `semantic_ffi_test`, `semantic_stream_observer_test`, `linearize_test`, `code_test`); old lowerer tests 85 (`debruijn_resolve_test`, `debruijn_linearize_test`, `debruijn_register_compile_test`, `debruijn_vm_contract_test`, `debruijn_register_contract_test`, `debruijn_register_benchmark_test`); old transport tests 141. Total suite today 4574 deftests. Of the 91, `semantic_engine_test`, `semantic_ffi_test`, `semantic_stream_observer_test` are engine-level tests parameterised over the old kernel and should be **ported** to the new kernel, not deleted (coverage audit in 8f). `debruijn_vm_contract_test` (B0 normaliser) and `debruijn_register_contract_test` (R0 descriptor/address law) pin contract facts, not lowerer behaviour: port the descriptor and normaliser assertions to `debruijn_code_test`/`debruijn_register_code_test`, delete the lowerer legs.\n\n### 1.6 SHA-256 sites that move to BLAKE3\n\nProduction (all in `src/cljc/yin/vm/`, found by `grep -rn -iE 'sha-?256' src`):\n\n| Site | Survives? | Moves? |\n|---|---|---|\n| `debruijn_code.cljc:548` `descriptor-hash`, `:564` `image-hash` (H) | yes | yes |\n| `debruijn_register_code.cljc:258` `descriptor-hash`, `:269` `register-hash` (R) | yes | yes |\n| `debruijn.cljc:966` `dimension-hash`, `:1028` `node-hash` (projection) | yes (pipeline D5) | yes |\n| descriptor rows `:hash :sha256` at `debruijn_code.cljc:138`, `debruijn_register_code.cljc:200`, `debruijn.cljc:139-140` (and the `:node-hash` prose string) | yes | yes: becomes `:hash :blake3`; this is a second descriptor row change beside `:dim/lift-to` (Q4) |\n| docstrings `debruijn_code.cljc:552-560`, `debruijn_register_code.cljc:262`, `debruijn.cljc:1021` | | rewrite |\n| `dao/jing.cljc` `sha256`, `sha256-bytes`, registry `:sha256` | yes, explicitly mintable registry member (owner) | no |\n| `dao/jing/content/step.cljc:288` `{:algorithm :sha256}` option | yes | no |\n\nTests mentioning SHA-256 inside yin.vm (counts): `linker_test` 8, `engine_test` 4, `completion_test` 3, `debruijn_test` 2, `malformed_rows` 2, and 1 each in `vm_test`, `debruijn_register_benchmark_test` (dies), `debruijn_register_compile_test` (dies), `semantic_test` (dies), `ucf/checkpoint_test` (dies), `ucf/rows_checkpoint_test`, `linker/head_follow_test`. Each must be read: most mint a `sha256` address deliberately to test the registry seam and stay; those that compute H/R by hand move. `dao.*` tests (jing, btree, space) are out of scope.\n\nNo `jing/digest-string :sha256` call exists in yin.vm; the move is six `jing/sha256` to `jing/blake3` edits plus descriptor rows plus prose.\n\n### 1.7 Switch points\n\n| Switch | Today | Phase 8 |\n|---|---|---|\n| `linker/relowered` | `linker.cljc:2159-2177`: `vm/semantic-bytecode->ast` then `linearize/adapt` / `register-compile/adapt` | `(derive/stack-image (:vector (sr-linearize/project-rows tree)))`, `(derive/register-image …)`; `verified-policy-outcome` `:2233` `(= :yin.semantic/code format-kw)` keeps its branch |\n| `relowered-hashes`, `verify-same-root-pairing`, `fallback-fetch` | `linker.cljc:2457-2565` (old H/R request paths: `resolve/resolve` then `lower-stack`/`lower-register` from source datoms) | these are the \"old H/R request paths\" of §10 item 8: delete `relowered-hashes` and the `resolve` require; `verify-same-root-pairing` recomputes from A: `(derive/* (:vector (sr-linearize/project-datoms source-datoms)))`. Tests: `linker_test.cljc:1969-1980`, `linker_manifest_test.cljc:740-743` |\n| `checked-derivation` | `linker.cljc:2252-2262`: record input must be the tree | de Bruijn records: input is A (the semantic derivation's output); semantic record unchanged (§7.6) |\n| `implemented-profiles` | `linker.cljc:952-958`; profile maps `:930-948` keep `\"stack-lowering\"`/`\"register-lowering\"` (ruling 2) | docstrings only |\n| `semantic-format` / `semantic-register-format` | `linker.cljc:695-731`; `interpreter.cljc:84-93` holds 7 records; `serve_test.cljc:446-452` asserts 7 | one record `semantic-format` with `:format :yin.semantic/code :contract \"v4\"` and the v4 scanners; `interpreter/formats` holds 6; `serve_test` asserts 6 |\n| `publish-closure!` | `publish.cljc:115-213`: `linearize/lower-rows` for the semantic vector, `dl/adapt`/`rc/adapt` on named datoms for H/R, `derive-record tree-address output` for all three, index `{h h-addr r r-addr}` `:189` | `sem-vector` from `sr-linearize/project-rows tree`; `h-image`/`r-image` from `derive` over that vector; records `A -> H`, `A -> R` (`:yin.ledger/input` = `sem`); index `{:yin.debruijn.code h-addr :yin.debruijn.register r-addr}`; contracts `\"v4\"` |\n| `address-attribute`/`index-from-datoms` | `linker.cljc:965-990` | re-minted; keyed by format |\n| REPL kernel table | `repl.cljc:143-146` `vm-constructors` (`semantic/create-vm`, `stack/create-vm`, `register/create-vm`), `:224-240` `program-loaders` (`linearize/rows-loader semantic/load-vector`; `debruijn-linearize/adapt`, `register-compile/adapt` over `packet->named-datoms`) | `:semantic` to the new `create-vm` and `load-vector` over `(:vector (sr-linearize/project-rows (macro/packet->row-set packet)))`; `:stack`/`:register` to `derive/stack-image`/`register-image` over the same vector; `packet->named-datoms` `:150-156` deleted; closes the REPL custody gap (UCF §7.11 note at `universal-continuation-format.md:3347`) |\n| `ledger/lowering-profile` and the derivation check | `ledger.cljc:40-50` (`\"ast-to-bytecode\"`, contract `\"v3\"`), `:194-204` (`code/well-formed-vector?`, `linearize/lower-rows`) | contract `\"v4\"`; check uses `sr-code/well-formed?` and `sr-linearize/project-rows` |\n| the `raise` dependency | the phrase comes from the frozen design (§10 item 8, F14 insertion point at `derivations.md:1267`); no `raise` symbol exists in `semantic_register*.cljc` or `linker.cljc` today | I read it as the old kernel's thrown-`ex-info` error path that `linker/relowered` swallows (`:2177` `catch … nil`) and `relowered-hashes` swallows (`:2466`); it leaves with those functions. Noted so the implementer does not hunt for a function named `raise` |\n\n### 1.8 Build tooling, Dart and docs touched by the rename\n\n- `src/dev/subsystems.edn` matches by prefix `yin.vm` and `yin.vm.ucf`; no change needed. `shadow-cljs.edn` auto-discovers `*-test`; no allowlist.\n- ClojureDart writes `lib/cljd-out/yin/vm/semantic_register.dart` and `semantic_register/*.dart`; after the rename, stale `.dart` files for the deleted namespaces remain in `lib/cljd-out/` on every machine that built before (laptop, shiva, jupiter). The Dart peer build (`bb.edn:18-25`, `dart compile exe lib/cljd-out/yin/repl/slice-peer.dart`) imports by path, so a stale `semantic.dart` from the OLD kernel colliding with the NEW `semantic.dart` is the concrete Dart risk (§4).\n- Docs that must change: the 13 files above; `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md` (836 lines, describes the v3 machine; 25 citers including `src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc`, `repl.cljc`, `ucf.cljc`, `docs/agents/format.md`); `yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md` (new revision record for `\"v4\"` as the one semantic contract); `yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:190, :390, :651, :3225, :3347` (phase-8 forward references); `yin.vm.linker.md:222-224` (§3 amendment); `yin.vm.debruijn.stack.md`, `yin.vm.debruijn.register.md`, `yin.vm.debruijn.targets.md` (SHA-256 prose, lowerer names); `semantic-register-vm.md` status header `:1-11` (\"working name… takes the name `yin.vm.semantic`\"); `derivations.md` §7.4-§8.3 PROPOSED to DECIDED/DONE; `continuation.md` §8.4; `docs/agents/build-n-test.md` if any lane names change (none expected).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Design questions phase 8 forces\n\nEach with recommendation and cost. OWNER marks a question only the owner can close.\n\n**Q1 (OWNER). Who edits the 10 C4 files, and when.** The cutover cannot compile without rewriting `linked_harness.cljc:105-120` and the ten Python tests that require the old kernel, `dl`, `rc` and `handoff`, and without regenerating `manifest-golden`, `pysp-manifest-golden` and verifying the literal-address pins. Options: (a) this track edits them in slice 8f under a frozen C4 branch state the owner declares (the other orchestrator pauses commits to those files for the slice's duration); (b) the other orchestrator ports them against a published 8c/8e API on a C4 branch, and 8f merges both. Recommendation: (a), because the change is mechanical (the harness's three backend lambdas and the loader) and a two-party edit of one file is the worst conflict shape; cost half a day plus the C4 slow lane (hours on a laptop; shiva/jupiter preferred). The owner must also say whether the C4 goldens are \"ours to move\" (derivations §8.1 says publish twice and diff; the review must show the diff).\n\n**Q2 (OWNER). Do versions 0/1/2, `handoff.cljc` and `checkpoint.cljc` die in this phase.** The brief says yes (\"leave at phase 8\"). The inventory confirms zero production requirers and 141 tests. Recommendation: yes, in one slice (8d), because keeping a 3783-line codec nobody produces is the legacy the no-compat rule forbids, and because its tests pin the old kernel and so block 8f. Cost: 1.5 days (mostly porting the helpers the new tests borrow from `lift_support`/`v2_support`). The one thing lost is the only existing test of the holder's acceptance of a version-2 body as a refusal; add one row to `rows_test` that a version-2 or version-0 header is `:yin.k/undecodable` (the holder path never dispatched on version; confirm, do not invent).\n\n**Q3. Rename mechanics for a name that other tracks and docs cite.** Recommendation: two commits inside slice 8f. Commit 1: `git mv` of 8 source and 16 test files plus `ns` forms, the `:yin.semantic-register/code` keyword to `:yin.semantic/code`, alias `sr` kept where it reads well; no logic change. Commit 2: the 56 requirers' API rewrite. Reviewers diff commit 1 with `--find-renames` and read only commit 2. Docs are a third commit (8g). Keep `yin.vm.semantic-register.*` nowhere, no alias namespace (no compat shim). Cost: 2 days codex, mostly the 58 `vm-load-program` sites.\n\n**Q4. The descriptor change is two rows, not one.** Derivations §8.1-8.2 say the printed descriptor \"differs from the previous one only by the removed `:dim/lift-to` row\", but the BLAKE3 ruling also flips the `:hash :sha256` row (`debruijn_code.cljc:138`, `debruijn_register_code.cljc:200`, `debruijn.cljc:139-140`). Recommendation: amend §8.1's mechanical check to \"differs by exactly the removed `:dim/lift-to` row and the `:hash` value\", and compute descriptor hashes with `jing/blake3`. No version bump (ruling 2 covers both as regeneration). Cost: zero beyond the doc line; flagged because a reviewer applying §8.1 literally would refuse the slice.\n\n**Q5. What replaces `yin.vm.code` for `completion.cljc` and `materialize-vector!`.** `completion.cljc:206, :256` project a v3 vector to qualified rows for the completion fold; `publish.cljc` materialises the semantic vector through `code/materialize-vector!`. Recommendation: `materialize-vector!` is one `jing/materialize!` of the vector and moves into `sr-code`; completion gets `sr-code`'s row projection (or `sr-linearize`'s) under the same name. Decide at 8c review whether `completion.cljc` needs a v4 projection at all or whether `completion_test` (3 SHA-256 sites) is testing a dead path. Cost: half a day.\n\n**Q6 (OWNER). Fate of `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md`.** It describes the v3 machine that is deleted. Options: (a) delete it and move the §1 stance and the `\"v2\"`/`\"v3\"` revision history into `yin.vm.ucf-revisions.md` §3-5; (b) rewrite its header to \"historical, superseded by `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md`\", keep body; (c) rename `yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.md` to `yin.vm.semantic.md` and delete the old. The frozen design's own header says it \"takes the name `yin.vm.semantic`\". Recommendation: (c) plus a one-paragraph history block, because a doc named after a namespace that no longer exists is the same legacy as a shim. Cost: 1 day of doc work in 8g including the 25 citers.\n\n**Q7. The old harness and stage tests of the old kernel.** `engine_gate_test`, `semantic_engine_test`, `semantic_ffi_test`, `semantic_stream_observer_test`, `stream_poll_test`, `telemetry_test`, `heap_reclamation_test` etc. exercise the engine through the old kernel. Recommendation: port, do not delete; the new kernel is the same engine's client. Slice 8f carries a coverage audit: list every deftest in deleted files, and for each name a surviving deftest or write one. Reviewer gate: the suite's deftest count after 8f is at least today's minus the 85 + 141 + (91 - ported) that are tests of deleted code, documented in the slice report with the list. Cost: in 8f's 2 days.\n\n**Q8. Who regenerates the C4 goldens and the literal-address pins.** Covered by Q1; the mechanical check is derivations §8.1 row 6 (publish twice, diff the manifest map). If the five `int_literal_test` and `float_address_test` pins are v3 vector addresses they die; if tree or v4 addresses they hold. Decided at 8f by printing them.\n\n**Q9. Deletion order (irreversible).** Fixed by this plan: 8a pins and regenerates goldens and moves the H descriptor golden; 8b moves BLAKE3; 8c switches production callers; 8d deletes the old transport; 8e deletes the three old lowerers; 8f deletes the old kernel and renames; 8g documents. Nothing is deleted before its replacement is green on three hosts in the same slice or earlier.\n\n**Q10. `derive_test`'s `:old` oracle legs** (`:150-160`, `:438-447`, `:612-630` `c4-backends :stack-old/:register-old`). These compare new lowerings against the old lowerers and die with them (8e). The execution oracle then compares `:stack-new`/`:register-new` against the walker and the semantic kernel only (§6.1 already names the walker as oracle). Recommendation: accept; the old lowerers' answers are already pinned as the §6.3 goldens.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Slicing\n\nEstimates: codex implementation days; review rounds 2-4 per slice (Fable/opus, non-author). Hosts: every slice runs the quick tier on JVM per iteration (`bb test:sub:clj yin.vm` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`), `bb test:changed` on three hosts at landing (G2). Slow tier (`bb test:all`), C4 suite with `--slow`, and three hosts once at the phase boundary (8f/8g).\n\n| Slice | Files | Depends on | Acceptance (named tests) | Hosts | Codex | Reviews |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **8a Descriptors and goldens** | `debruijn_code.cljc:145`, `debruijn_register_code.cljc:204` (drop `:dim/lift-to`), `debruijn_code_test.cljc:364-368` (drop two lift assertions, pin descriptor hash), `derive_test.cljc:240, :419-428` (regenerate), `linker_test.cljc:253-260, :522-531` (helpers to `derive`, regenerate), `debruijn_register_code_test.cljc:7`, `debruijn_register_compile_test.cljc:446` (dup golden deleted), `stack_parity_test.cljc:321-347` (producer to `derive`, regenerate) | none | `debruijn-code-test/descriptor-declares-the-contract` (now pins hash), `derive-test/{stack-goldens,register-goldens,stack-determinism,register-determinism,lexical-address-law,lexical-address-law-stack}`, `linker-test/pinned-identities-are-host-independent`, `stack-parity-test/*`; reviewer recomputes every new constant from the printed image per §8.1 | 3 | 1 d | 2 |\n| **8b BLAKE3** | `debruijn_code.cljc:138, :548, :564`, `debruijn_register_code.cljc:200, :258, :269`, `debruijn.cljc:139-140, :966, :1028` + docstrings; `debruijn_test.cljc:136, :933, :1300-1306`; re-pin 8a's goldens once more (or merge 8a+8b so goldens move once: **recommend merging 8a and 8b into one slice \"8ab\"** so every H/R constant regenerates exactly once) | 8a | same as 8a plus `debruijn-test/*`, `pipeline-test/*`; descriptor-hash recomputed by `(jing/blake3 (dc/encode-scalar <printed descriptor>))` | 3 | 0.5 d | 2 |\n| **8c Production pipeline to A** | `linker.cljc:46-52` requires, `:695-731` one `semantic-format` (v4 scanners), `:2159-2177` `relowered`, `:2252-2320` `checked-derivation` (de Bruijn input = A), `:2457-2565` pairing paths; `publish.cljc:5-17, :159-213`; `ledger.cljc:40-50, :194-204`; `vm.cljc:266-269` `\"v4\"`, `:1755` footprint key; `ucf.cljc:47-68`; `completion.cljc:206, :256` (Q5); `interpreter.cljc:84-93`; `repl.cljc:27-41, :143-156, :224-240`; `rows.cljc:2118`, `semantic_register.cljc:410-456` keyword to `:yin.semantic/code` (so the keyword moves once, here, not in 8f); tests `linker_test`, `linker_manifest_test:740`, `linker_require_test`, `linker/publish_test`, `repl/serve_test:446-452`, `semantic_register/link_test`, `repl_test`, `ucf_test` (v4 vectors), `ledger_test` | 8ab | `linker-test/*` incl. `pinned-identities-are-host-independent`, `verify-same-root-pairing` tests `:1969-1980`, `linker-manifest-test/*`, `linker-require-test/*`, `publish-test/*` (manifest shape: contracts `\"v4\"`, three derivation records with de Bruijn input = A, index keyed by format), `serve-test/the-composition-registers-the-v4-format-record` (6 records), `repl-test/*`, `dht_process_test`, `remote_transfer_test`, `cross_host_transfer_test`; `derive_test/c4-execution-oracle` still green | 3 (JVM + Node REPL peer + Dart peer are all involved) | 3 d | 3-4 |\n| **8d Old transport out** | delete `handoff.cljc`, `checkpoint.cljc`, 15 test files, `lift_support.cljc`, `v2_support.cljc`, `checkpoint_fixtures.cljc`, 3 fixtures + minter; first move borrowed helpers into `rows_support.cljc` (new `frame_test`, `layout_test`, `semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test` requires rewired); `int_heap_test.cljc:241`, `int_contract_test.cljc:483` to the holder path (C4 files: Q1) | none (can run concurrently with 8ab/8c) | `rows-test/*`, `rows-checkpoint-test/*`, `compose-rows-test/*` (41), `fencing-rows-test`, `stage-e-rows-test`, `stage_e_process_test`, `holder/*`, `authority/*`, `semantic-register/safepoint-harness-test/*` (26), `frame-test/*` (10), `layout-test/*` (12); one new refusal row for a version-0/2 header | 3 | 1.5 d | 2-3 |\n| **8e Old lowerers out** | delete `debruijn_resolve.cljc`, `debruijn_linearize.cljc`, `debruijn_register_compile.cljc`, their 3 tests, `debruijn_register_benchmark_test`; port the contract facts of `debruijn_vm_contract_test`/`debruijn_register_contract_test` to `debruijn_code_test`/`debruijn_register_code_test`, delete the rest; `test_utils.cljc:24, :200-210` (`resolved-of` becomes `derive` over the projected vector); `derive_test` `:old` legs out (Q10); every test that builds a kernel through `dl/adapt`/`rc/adapt` (37 files, the 10 C4 files among them: Q1) switches to `derive/stack-image`/`register-image` over `(:vector (sr-linearize/project ast))` | 8c (production no longer calls them) | the full `yin.vm` subsystem quick tier; `derive-test/*` incl. `c4-execution-oracle` (`--slow`); `debruijn/register_test`, `debruijn/stack_*_test`; kondo clean (`clj -M:kondo`) with no unresolved namespace | 3 | 2 d | 2-3 |\n| **8f Old kernel out, rename, C4 gate** | delete `semantic.cljc`, `linearize.cljc`, `code.cljc` (what remains after 8c) and `semantic_test`, `linearize_test`, `code_test`; port `semantic_engine_test`, `semantic_ffi_test`, `semantic_stream_observer_test` to the new kernel; commit 1 `git mv` `semantic_register*` to `semantic*` and `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/` to `test/yin/vm/semantic/`; commit 2 rewrite the 56 requirers (`vm-load-program` to `load-ast`/`load-vector`); demos (`continuation_handoff.cljc`, `continuation_stream.cljs`, `register_bench_cljd.cljd`, `yin_vm_bench`) ported; `linked_harness.cljc:105-120`; C4 goldens regenerated and literal pins verified (Q1, Q8); coverage audit list | 8c, 8d, 8e | **phase gate**: `bb test:all` three hosts (slow tier), `bb test:sub yang.python --slow` (C4 suite: `import_test`, `linked_prelude_test`, `linked_safepoint_test`, `safepoint_test`, `dht_import_test`, `e2e_test`, `e2e_c2_test`, `prelude_parity_test`) over newly published images = the install-child gate of derivations §6.1.4 (c); `repl-test/*`, `dht_process_test`, `store_write_audit_test` (allowlist touches); deftest-count audit per Q7; kondo clean; `lib/cljd-out` cleaned before the Dart lane | 3 + shiva/jupiter for the slow tier | 3 d | 3-4 |\n| **8g Documentation** | the 13 design docs, `yin.vm.semantic.md` fate (Q6), `ucf-revisions.md` record, `linker.md` §3, `build-n-test.md` if needed, the frozen design's §10 item 8 marked complete, `derivations.md` §7.4-8.3 and `continuation.md` §8.4 statuses, `debruijn.*.md` SHA-256 prose | 8f | Architect doc review; `grep -r 'yin.vm.semantic-register' docs src test` returns only historical/dated statements; `grep -rn 'sha256' src/cljc/yin/vm` returns none | n/a | 1.5 d | 2 |\n\nOrdering and concurrency:\n\n| | needs | concurrent with |\n|---|---|---|\n| 8ab | nothing | 8d |\n| 8c | 8ab | 8d |\n| 8d | nothing | 8ab, 8c |\n| 8e | 8c | nothing (touches 37 test files; serialize) |\n| 8f | 8c, 8d, 8e | nothing |\n| 8g | 8f | nothing (docs only; may start drafting during 8f review) |\n\nGate list: quick tier (`bb test:sub:clj yin.vm` per iteration, `bb test:changed` three hosts at landing) for every slice; `bb test:sub yin.vm yin.vm.ucf` at 8c and 8e checkpoints; the slow tier once at 8f on shiva/jupiter plus the C4 suite with `--slow`; three hosts at every landing from 8ab up because the regenerated constants must agree on JVM, Node and Dart (that is what `pinned-identities-are-host-independent` and `register-determinism` exist for).\n\nBranching: `srvm-phase8` off `srvm-phase7` (after phase 7 lands on master, rebase once); each slice a branch `srvm-phase8a` etc. merged into `srvm-phase8`; `srvm-phase8` lands on master only after 8g.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Risks and mitigations\n\n1. **Mass rename collides with the other orchestrator.** 56 files, 10 of them C4's, and `linked_harness.cljc` is the shared hinge. Mitigation: Q1 ruling; freeze window announced via collab/owner relay before 8e starts; 8e and 8f edit C4 files only as mechanical API substitution with no behavioural change; the C4 suite over new images is the proof. Never touch `datomworld-c4-*` worktrees (none exist in `git worktree list` today; the C4 work seems to live in the main checkout's branches).\n2. **Accidental coverage loss when deleting 317 deftests.** Mitigation: Q7 audit list in the 8f report (deleted test name, reason: tests deleted code / ported to X / replaced by Y); port the engine-level tests; reviewer refuses 8f without the list. Count today: 4574.\n3. **Hash regeneration drift.** Three regeneration moments collapse into one (8ab merged) so each H/R constant moves once; §8.1's mechanical check is applied by the reviewer on printed intermediates (image, descriptor, manifest map); A addresses in `corpus.cljc` (32) and `rows_test` (20) and the 7a/7b fixtures are asserted unchanged as a negative gate. Descriptor diff is exactly two rows (Q4).\n4. **Dart name resolution on rename.** The new `yin.vm.semantic` compiles to `lib/cljd-out/yin/vm/semantic.dart`, the same path the old kernel occupied; a stale build cache or a stale `semantic_register.dart` referenced by a stale `semantic_register/*.dart` can shadow or double-define. Mitigation: 8f's Dart lane starts with a clean `lib/cljd-out` (and the same on shiva/jupiter before their lanes); the known cljd traps (reader-conditional order, `[_ _]` protocol params, chunked `for`, unary minus) are re-checked by the three-host gate, not by inspection. Also the file rename changes the cljd build's import graph; the Dart peer binary must be rebuilt (`bb build:yin-repl-peer`) before `dht_process_test`.\n5. **The REPL custody gap closes by rename, but the REPL also loses `packet->named-datoms`.** `repl.cljc:150-156` feeds the old lowerers from named datoms; after 8c the stack/register evaluators derive from A. A REPL session that `def`s across inputs relies on `append-stack-image`/`append-register-image` (`:159-204`) keeping earlier images; `derive` produces images with the same `:segment`/`:bodies` shape, but `absolute-pc` lookups use `dcode/image-hash`, which moves under BLAKE3 in the same window. Mitigation: `repl_test` and `dht_process_test` in 8c's acceptance; run the REPL smoke on Node (`target/yin-repl.js`) in 8c.\n6. **`completion.cljc` and the v3 table (Q5)** may hide a dependency on v3 row shapes in the completion fold; discovered at 8c. Mitigation: `completion_test` in 8c acceptance; if the fold needs a v4 projection that does not exist, 8c's report says so and 8e waits.\n7. **Review-round inflation.** Phases 5-7 averaged 2-4 rounds; 8c and 8f are the two slices with the most files and the most hosts. Mitigation: split 8f into two commits (Q3) so the reviewer reads the rename as moves; keep `--find-renames` diffs in the report.\n8. **Credits.** Codex implements (gpt-6.1-sol, workspace-write); Fable/opus review only. If codex is out, implementation waits (owner rule); do not move implementation to opus without a ruling.\n\n**Rollback story.** Every slice is a branch; `srvm-phase8` is not merged to master until 8g. Rolling back a slice is deleting its branch and re-running the predecessor's quick tier. Rolling back after master lands is a `git revert` of the one merge commit; nothing is persisted outside git (dev-only repo, no deployed stores, owner rule). The regenerated goldens and C4 manifests are recomputable from the printed intermediates in each slice report, so no golden is ever \"lost\": the old values are in git history and the new ones in the report.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Honest total estimate\n\n| | Codex days | Review rounds | Calendar |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 8ab | 1.5 | 2-3 | 2-3 days |\n| 8c | 3 | 3-4 | 5-6 days |\n| 8d (concurrent) | 1.5 | 2-3 | within 8ab+8c |\n| 8e | 2 | 2-3 | 3-4 days |\n| 8f | 3 | 3-4 | 5-6 days + slow tier (JVM 20+ min, Node/Dart ~5 min each, C4 slow sequence hours on a laptop; use shiva/jupiter) |\n| 8g | 1.5 | 2 | 2-3 days |\n| **Total** | **12.5 (range 11-15)** | **14-19 rounds** | **4-5 weeks** with 8d overlapped; 6 weeks if every slice hits 4 rounds or codex credits lapse |\n\nNot counted: the owner's own time on Q1-Q6, and any C4-side rebase the other orchestrator needs after 8f.\n\n---\n\n## Verdict\n\n**NOT READY.** Blocking owner questions:\n\n- **Q1** who edits the 10 C4 files and the two C4 manifest goldens, and the freeze window.\n- **Q2** confirm versions 0/1/2, `handoff.cljc` and `checkpoint.cljc` die in 8d (the brief implies yes; the inventory found no production requirer, so a yes costs nothing).\n- **Q4** accept the two-row descriptor diff (lift-to removed, `:hash :blake3`) as the amended §8.1 check.\n- **Q6** fate of `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md` (recommend: the frozen design takes the name, old doc deleted, history block kept).\n\nQ3, Q5, Q7-Q10 are recommendations the orchestrator can adopt without an owner ruling. On Q1, Q2, Q4, Q6 answered, slice 8ab can be briefed to codex the same day; 8d can be briefed in parallel on Q2 alone.\n# Multihash-Style Content Addressing for DaoJing and yin.vm\n\nStatus: landed on master (2026-09-25, merge e149aa31)\n\n## Decision\n\nDaoJing will support multiple hash algorithms as permanent, first-class registry members. The initial registry contains:\n\n- BLAKE3-256, the default minting algorithm; and\n- SHA-256, an equally supported algorithm available through explicit minting.\n\nSHA-256 is not deprecated, transitional, or a compatibility fallback. A caller may intentionally mint SHA-256 content at any time:\n\n```clojure\n(segment-key value {:algorithm :sha256})\n```\n\nA DaoJing store may ordinarily contain addresses produced by both algorithms. Parsing, verification, remote transfer, DHT storage, and cache hydration dispatch according to the algorithm carried by each address.\n\nThere is no migration or backward-compatibility project. Datom.world has no deployed DaoJing stores, published manifests, externally held addresses, or legacy systems. The registry and BLAKE3 default therefore land as one clean contract: fixtures and development stores are regenerated rather than migrated.\n\nBLAKE3 is pinned to its standard unkeyed 256-bit output. Variable-length BLAKE3 output, keyed hashing, and derivation-key modes are different algorithms and are not admitted under the `blake3` identifier.\n\n## Address format\n\nA segment address identifies the hash algorithm and carries its digest:\n\n```text\n:segment/<algorithm-id>-<lowercase-hex-digest>\n```\n\nThe initial canonical forms are:\n\n```text\n:segment/blake3-<64 lowercase hex>\n:segment/sha256-<64 lowercase hex>\n```\n\nThis is the project's EDN-readable multihash representation. The algorithm identifier is self-describing; the digest length and validation rules come from the corresponding registry entry.\n\nThe format deliberately does not reproduce IPFS's binary varint multihash representation. DaoJing addresses cross EDN boundaries and must survive `pr-str` followed by EDN reading unchanged.\n\nThere is one current canonical encoder. The address does not identify it. A future canonical-encoding change is a clean break that regenerates every address and store rather than retaining earlier addresses.\n\n## Invariants\n\n- An address is derived solely from the current canonical bytes and the selected hash algorithm.\n- BLAKE3 and SHA-256 are permanent, first-class algorithm-registry members.\n- BLAKE3 is the default for implicit minting; SHA-256 minting is explicit and ordinary.\n- Address verification always uses the algorithm carried by the address, never the current minting default.\n- Verification is address-directed; minting primitives are not validators.\n- Copying content already named by an address preserves the algorithm carried by that address.\n- The algorithm registry is closed and immutable. There is no runtime registration or hidden mutable state.\n- Adding or removing a registry entry is an explicit source and contract change.\n- Unknown algorithms, malformed lengths, uppercase hex, and non-hex digests fail closed.\n- Digest length belongs to the algorithm registry rather than to the parser. Both initial algorithms produce 32-byte digests.\n- A bare digest is not a durable content identifier. Persisted identities use an algorithm-qualified address.\n- Every supported algorithm/digest pair has exactly one canonical address spelling.\n- Canonical encoding precedes algorithm selection: both algorithms hash the same `canonical-bytes` result for a given value.\n- Changing canonical encoding changes every digest and is handled as a clean break, not as address compatibility.\n- Encoder refusal occurs before hashing and is not an algorithm failure.\n\n## DaoJing design\n\n### Closed algorithm registry\n\n`dao.jing` owns a small immutable registry:\n\n```clojure\n{:blake3 {:address-id \"blake3\"\n          :digest-bytes 32\n          ...}\n :sha256 {:address-id \"sha256\"\n          :digest-bytes 32\n          ...}}\n```\n\nEach entry defines:\n\n- its internal keyword;\n- its canonical address identifier;\n- its digest length;\n- its byte-digest implementation; and\n- enough formatting metadata to validate its lowercase hexadecimal result.\n\nThe registry contains no runtime extension mechanism. Supporting a future algorithm requires a reviewed source change, a pinned identifier, provider choices for every host, official vectors, and cross-host address fixtures.\n\nThe default is explicit immutable data:\n\n```clojure\ndefault-hash-algorithm ; => :blake3\n```\n\nThe default controls implicit minting only. It is never consulted to verify an existing address.\n\nAlgorithm address identifiers are lowercase ASCII alphanumeric strings matching `[a-z0-9]+`. The parser splits the keyword name on the first `-` to extract the algorithm identifier and digest, then performs an exact registry lookup. There is no ambiguous prefix matching.\n\n### Public digest primitives\n\nAdd algorithm-neutral primitives:\n\n```clojure\n(digest-bytes algorithm bytes) ; lowercase hex\n(digest-string algorithm s)    ; UTF-8, then digest-bytes\n```\n\nUnknown algorithms throw with the requested identifier.\n\nKeep explicit functions where they make fixed contracts readable:\n\n```clojure\n(sha256 s)\n(sha256-bytes bytes)\n(blake3 s)\n(blake3-bytes bytes)\n```\n\nThese functions never follow the default. `sha256` remains useful to VM and DHT contracts independently of its membership in the DaoJing address registry.\n\n### `canonical-bytes`\n\nThe signature remains:\n\n```clojure\n(canonical-bytes value)\n```\n\nIt returns the canonical CBOR payload bytes of the value: `canonical-bytes`\ndelegates to `dao.jing.cbor/encode`, the pinned profile of\n`dao.jing.cbor.md`, and every digest input is those bytes. The\norder-normalized print encoder that once stood here is gone (clean break,\n2026-09-24).\n\nNo algorithm-specific canonicalization is permitted. BLAKE3 and SHA-256 receive identical bytes for the same value.\n\nThere is exactly one encoder; the CBOR codec did not add a second\ncoexisting encoder and did not alter this function's public role.\n\n### `content-hash`\n\nRetain:\n\n```clojure\n(content-hash value)\n```\n\nIt hashes `canonical-bytes` with BLAKE3.\n\nAdd explicit algorithm selection:\n\n```clojure\n(content-hash value {:algorithm :sha256})\n```\n\nImplementation is always:\n\n```text\ncanonical-bytes(value)\n        │\n        ▼\ndigest-bytes(algorithm, bytes)\n```\n\nThere must be one canonical byte stream and one digest operation.\n\nBecause the result remains bare hexadecimal text, `content-hash` is suitable for local calculations and explicitly versioned contracts. Newly persisted standalone identity fields use `segment-key` or another algorithm-qualified envelope.\n\n### `segment-key`\n\nRetain:\n\n```clojure\n(segment-key value)\n```\n\nAdd:\n\n```clojure\n(segment-key value {:algorithm :sha256})\n```\n\nThe one-argument form selects `default-hash-algorithm`.\n\nFormatting is data-driven by the selected registry entry:\n\n```clojure\n(keyword \"segment\"\n         (str algorithm-id \"-\" digest))\n```\n\nThe namespace is exactly `segment`. Algorithm identifiers and digest text are lowercase ASCII, and every output must survive `pr-str` and EDN reading unchanged.\n\n### Address parsing\n\nAdd one authoritative parser:\n\n```clojure\n(parse-segment-address address)\n;; => {:algorithm :blake3\n;;     :digest \"...\"\n;;     :canonical address}\n;; or nil\n```\n\nAll accessors delegate to it:\n\n```clojure\n(segment-address? address)\n(segment-algorithm address)\n(segment-hash address)\n```\n\n`segment-address?` remains a total predicate. `segment-algorithm` and `segment-hash` throw on invalid input.\n\nThe parser:\n\n1. requires a keyword in the `segment` namespace;\n2. splits the keyword name on the first `-` into algorithm identifier and digest strings;\n3. looks up the algorithm identifier in the registered algorithms table;\n4. validates digest length from that algorithm's registry entry;\n5. requires lowercase hexadecimal text;\n6. reconstructs the canonical address; and\n7. rejects unknown algorithms and alternative spellings.\n\nThe parser matches registered identifiers rather than accepting arbitrary prefixes. A syntactically plausible but unregistered algorithm is invalid.\n\n### `segment-matches?`\n\nAdd:\n\n```clojure\n(segment-matches? address payload)\n```\n\nIt:\n\n1. parses the address;\n2. obtains `canonical-bytes` for the payload;\n3. selects the algorithm carried by the address;\n4. hashes the bytes;\n5. compares the digest; and\n6. confirms that reformatting the parsed pair reproduces the canonical address.\n\nThis dispatch is the core multihash behavior. A store may contain an explicitly minted SHA-256 object and a default-minted BLAKE3 object at the same time, and each verifies under its own algorithm.\n\nMalformed, unsupported, noncanonical, or mismatched addresses return false. Canonical-encoder refusal also returns false, making `segment-matches?` a total verification predicate. Minting operations such as `materialize!` and `segment-key` continue to throw on encoder refusal.\n\nOutside `dao.jing`, this is the normal operation for validating an address/payload pair. Callers do not manually compare `segment-hash` with `content-hash`, nor re-mint under the default with `segment-key`.\n\n### `materialize!`\n\nRetain:\n\n```clojure\n(materialize! handle payload)\n```\n\nAdd:\n\n```clojure\n(materialize! handle payload {:algorithm :sha256})\n```\n\nThe one-argument form mints with BLAKE3. The explicit form supports any registered algorithm.\n\nOn `:present`, verify the stored payload with:\n\n```clojure\n(segment-matches? address stored)\n```\n\nDo not use the default algorithm to validate it.\n\nWhen copying content already named by an address into another store, derive the minting algorithm from that address:\n\n```clojure\n(materialize! destination payload\n  {:algorithm (segment-algorithm address)})\n```\n\nThe returned address must equal the source address. This is required for ordinary multi-algorithm operation: a SHA-256 object fetched from a peer remains a SHA-256 object when cached, even though BLAKE3 is the default for unrelated new content.\n\n### `get`\n\nThe signature remains:\n\n```clojure\n(get handle address not-found)\n```\n\nIt accepts every address recognized by `segment-address?`, including both initial algorithms. It rejects arbitrary keys before consulting the backend.\n\n`get` need not rehash every successful read. Existing backend insertion and replay checks, plus higher-level verified readers, retain their responsibilities. A consumer that verifies fetched content uses `segment-matches?`.\n\nHistorical, registry era: the paragraph above describes the registry-era\nread policy, written before the canonical CBOR swap. The current rule in\n`dao.jing.cbor.md` is stricter: `get` hash-verifies every read against the\nrequested address before decoding.\n\n### File-backend codec round-trip (historical, registry era)\n\nThis section describes the registry-era printer codec and its round-trip\ncheck, both removed by the canonical CBOR swap. File replay now performs\nstrict CBOR validation of each frame and payload; see `dao.jing.cbor.md`\n(*Memory and files*).\n\n`dao.jing.file/validate-codec-round-trip!` is not an address/payload validator. It compares a payload's identity with its own `pr-str`/EDN round trip to ensure that the file codec does not discard address-significant information.\n\nIt does not use `segment-matches?`. Both sides must be hashed with the algorithm selected for the pending address:\n\n```clojure\n(content-hash payload  {:algorithm algorithm})\n(content-hash replayed {:algorithm algorithm})\n```\n\nThis makes explicit SHA-256 materialization and default BLAKE3 materialization obey the same file-codec rule.\n\n## Clean introduction\n\nThe registry is introduced without a migration phase.\n\nThere are no deployed DaoJing stores or published addresses to preserve. The implementation therefore changes the contract atomically:\n\n- BLAKE3 becomes the one-argument minting default immediately.\n- Explicit SHA-256 minting is available immediately as an ordinary registry operation.\n- Existing development fixtures are regenerated.\n- Existing file stores are not upgraded or read through a compatibility path.\n- Existing checkpoint shapes are replaced rather than dual-read.\n- All peers in a development composition run the new address contract together.\n- No old-address alias, graph migration, dual checkpoint schema, or format negotiation is added.\n\nThe absence of migration machinery does not weaken multi-algorithm support. SHA-256 and BLAKE3 coexist because callers may intentionally select either after the registry lands, not because content from an earlier system is being carried forward.\n\n## Call-site audit and required dispositions\n\nHash use outside `dao.jing` falls into four classes:\n\n1. frozen non-Jing hash contracts;\n2. minting new content;\n3. validating content against an existing address; and\n4. copying addressed content while preserving its selected algorithm.\n\nThe classification is a maintained design artifact. Minting and verification must not be conflated.\n\n### Direct `jing/sha256` consumers\n\nThe current tree contains five direct consumers:\n\n- `yin.vm.debruijn_code/descriptor-hash`\n- `yin.vm.debruijn_code/image-hash`\n- `yin.vm.debruijn/dimension-hash`, a `def` computed once at namespace load\n- `yin.vm.debruijn/node-hash`\n- `dao.jing.dht/node-id`\n\nThese calls do not follow DaoJing's default.\n\nThe four de Bruijn hashes are SHA-256 today and are VM-format contracts, not default selections. Owner decision 2026-10-12: the H and R checksums and the descriptor hashes move to BLAKE3 at the phase-8 cutover of the semantic-register VM (derivations design §8.2), where every golden is regenerated once. Until then they stay explicit SHA-256 and must not follow DaoJing's default.\n\nThe DHT node ID remains SHA-256 because it defines the DHT routing identity space rather than a content-address selection. Changing it is a separate DHT protocol decision.\n\nNeither disposition is a legacy-support exception. They are independent contract boundaries that happen to specify SHA-256.\n\nThe task that originated this plan named `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc` and register contract tests, but those files do not exist in the reviewed checkout. The register design nevertheless specifies R as SHA-256. Its implementation follows that frozen format contract when it lands.\n\n### Primitive-profile identifier\n\n`yin.vm/primitive-profile` constructs:\n\n```clojure\n:yin.k.pp/blake3-<digest of (content-hash description {:algorithm :blake3})>\n```\n\nThe profile digest is computed explicitly with BLAKE3 (owner decision 2026-10-12, with the capability seal and the owner tag), not by riding the default. The `blake3-` label stays truthful even if the default algorithm later changes. This is an explicit VM contract using a first-class registry algorithm, not a compatibility fallback.\n\nA future canonical-encoding clean break may change this digest because `content-hash` follows the one current canonical encoder. If primitive-profile identity must remain frozen across that break, its owning VM contract must first pin its own byte encoder rather than relying on DaoJing's changing content encoder.\n\n### DaoSpace schema identity\n\n`dao.space.index/checkpoint-candidate` currently persists a bare `:schema-hash`, and `restore` recomputes it using the ambient default. A bare digest is not algorithm-agile.\n\nReplace it outright with:\n\n```clojure\n:schema-address (jing/segment-key schema)\n```\n\n`restore` verifies the supplied schema with:\n\n```clojure\n(jing/segment-matches? schema-address schema)\n```\n\nThere is no fallback reader for the old `:schema-hash` field and no dual candidate shape. No existing checkpoint candidate requires one.\n\n### Address-directed validation sites\n\nThe following sites validate content against an address already supplied by stored data, a caller, or a peer. They use `segment-matches?` (the `dao.jing.remote` sites describe the code as it stands today; `dao.jing.remote` is deprecated whole and its successor `dao.jing.content` keeps the same checks, per `dao.stream.remote.md` section 8):\n\n- `dao.jing/materialize!` on `:present` read-back\n- `dao.jing.mem/validate-address-payload!`\n- `dao.jing.file/validate-address-payload!`\n- `dao.jing.remote/validate-address-payload!`\n- `dao.jing.remote.step` verification of a `:present` response\n- `dao.jing.dht/validate-address-payload!`\n- `dao.jing.dht` peer-fetched content verification before caching\n- `dao.jing.dht.node` store-request validation\n- `dao.data.btree.storage` optional fetched-blob verification in the ordinary KV reader\n- `dao.space.index/read-manifest`\n- `yin.vm.content` row fetch verification\n- `yin.vm.content` vector fetch verification\n- `yin.vm.macro` row-address verification\n- `yin.vm.semantic` claimed-segment verification\n- `yin.vm.completion` reconstructed-image address verification\n- `yin.vm.ledger` row-address verification\n- `yin.vm.ledger` output-address verification\n- `yin.vm.ledger` record/derivation verification\n- `yin.vm` semantic-bytecode row validation\n\nBackend sites currently compare `segment-hash` with `content-hash`; DaoSpace and yin.vm sites generally re-mint with one-argument `segment-key` and compare whole addresses. Both mechanisms incorrectly substitute minting defaults for address-directed verification.\n\nGeneration-only calls to `segment-key` remain one-argument calls when BLAKE3 is intended. Callers intentionally choosing SHA-256 pass `{:algorithm :sha256}`.\n\n### Address-directed mint sites\n\nFour copy paths receive content under an existing address and materialize or flush it into a local cache or remote store:\n\n- `dao.jing.dht/make-get`\n- `dao.data.btree.storage/hydrate!`, synchronous `pull!`\n- `dao.data.btree.storage/hydrate-async`, asynchronous `fetched!`\n- `dao.data.btree.storage/store-tree-async`, cache-minted blob flush to remote\n\nAfter verifying the payload, local cache paths materialize using the address-carried algorithm:\n\n```clojure\n(materialize! local payload\n  {:algorithm (segment-algorithm address)})\n```\n\nIn `store-tree-async`, the flush must supply the source address (and its algorithm) to the remote store via `put-content` or an explicit address-supplying operation rather than calling un-parameterized `materialize-async-fn` / `request-materialize`, which would incorrectly re-mint under the remote's ambient default.\n\nUsing one-argument `materialize!` would incorrectly convert an intentionally SHA-addressed object into a BLAKE3-addressed cache entry.\n\nThis is ordinary multihash correctness, not migration behavior.\n\n### Documentation-only SHA assumptions\n\nDocstrings and errors in `dao.jing`, `dao.jing.mem`, `dao.jing.file`, `dao.jing.dht`, `dao.jing.dht.kad`, `dao.space.index`, and `dao.jing.stream` that describe segment storage as SHA-only must be generalized.\n\nThe DHT may route by the carried 256-bit digest. Both initial algorithms produce 64 hexadecimal characters, and the full segment address remains the storage key. If a future registered algorithm has a different digest width, Kademlia normalization must be designed explicitly rather than assuming every registry entry is 256 bits.\n\n## yin.vm H and R disposition\n\nH and R remain explicitly SHA-256.\n\nFor the implemented stack format (until the phase-8 cutover moves it to BLAKE3):\n\n- keep `descriptor-hash` and `image-hash` on explicit SHA-256;\n- keep descriptor data declaring `:hash :sha256`;\n- do not repin the golden H corpus before the cutover;\n- add a regression proving that DaoJing's BLAKE3 default does not change H.\n\nThe older `yin.vm.debruijn` dimension and node hashes likewise remain explicit SHA-256 under their existing format contract until the same cutover.\n\nFor the planned register format:\n\n- implement `register-descriptor-hash` and `register-hash` with explicit SHA-256 now and BLAKE3 from the phase-8 cutover;\n- retain the documented R formula and descriptor declaration;\n- do not couple R to `default-hash-algorithm`.\n\nThis is neither compatibility support nor an application of DaoJing's multihash registry. H and R are executable-format identities with their own canonical encoders, descriptor hashes, contract versions, and pinned golden values. Changing them requires a VM format-version decision and complete re-pin under that process.\n\nJing addresses for stored stack or register images are separate identities. An image may have:\n\n- H or R, identifying its executable-format bytes; and\n- a DaoJing segment address, identifying the stored Jing value under its selected registry algorithm.\n\nThe two identities must not be conflated merely because both can use the same algorithm.\n\n## Host libraries\n\nUse the portability spike's validated BLAKE3 providers:\n\n- JVM: `io.github.rctcwyvrn/blake3` 1.3\n- ClojureScript/Node: `@noble/hashes` 2.4.0\n- ClojureDart: `blake3_dart` 1.0.0\n\nPin exact versions. A content-identity dependency must not use floating ranges.\n\nThe portability spike's cross-provider result left no durable repository artifact. The implementation therefore commits official-vector fixtures and the cross-provider digest table as the evidence of record.\n\nSHA-256 keeps its current per-host implementations, including the hand-written ClojureDart implementation. That code serves both the first-class SHA-256 registry entry and the explicit VM/DHT contracts.\n\n### Maintenance risks\n\nThe JVM BLAKE3 dependency is pure bytecode and MIT licensed but stale, with its Maven release dating to 2020. Acceptance requires:\n\n- source and license review;\n- official BLAKE3 known-answer tests;\n- chunk and tree-boundary vectors;\n- a cross-host fixture corpus;\n- isolation behind `digest-bytes`; and\n- a provider-replacement procedure that requires complete conformance before adoption.\n\nThe Dart dependency is pure Dart and passed official vectors, but has only one release and a short history. It receives the same fixture and replacement discipline. It is also the Dart host's first external cryptographic dependency, so dependency resolution and release-build execution are explicit gates.\n\n`@noble/hashes` is actively maintained, but its ESM/package integration must be exercised in the current Shadow-CLJS test and optimized-release builds.\n\nAny provider version or replacement that changes a pinned digest is rejected as nonconforming to the registered algorithm.\n\n## Sequencing with canonical CBOR\n\nThe algorithm-registry epic landed first, over the order-normalized print\nencoder of the time; the canonical CBOR clean break then replaced that\nencoder on 2026-09-24. Both are complete.\n\nThe registry epic established:\n\n- the flat multihash address grammar;\n- permanent BLAKE3 and SHA-256 entries;\n- BLAKE3 as the minting default;\n- explicit SHA-256 minting;\n- algorithm-directed verification; and\n- algorithm-preserving copy paths.\n\nCanonical CBOR was a separate clean-break epic, now landed. The codec in\n`dao.jing.cbor`, whose byte contract is frozen by\n`test/resources/dao/jing/cbor-v1.json`, is the addressing encoder:\n`canonical-bytes` is `dao.jing.cbor/encode`. The algorithm registry was\nunchanged by that landing:\n\n- BLAKE3 remains the default;\n- SHA-256 remains explicitly selectable;\n- the address grammar remains `:segment/<algorithm-id>-<digest>`;\n- every digest and every DaoJing address was regenerated from CBOR bytes\n  (`digest-table.edn`'s canonical rows, the psset fixtures, and every\n  pinned test hash);\n- development stores, fixtures, manifests, ASTs, and continuations were\n  rebuilt together; and\n- no printer-address reader, alias, graph migration, or encoding\n  negotiation was introduced.\n\nThe existing `dao.jing.cbor.md` \"Addressing and clean break\" ruling remains authoritative. This document changes its SHA-only algorithm assumption but does not alter its clean-break policy.\n\nThe print and CBOR value domains were not identical: the CBOR profile\nrefuses records, functions, host objects, characters, `#inst` and\n`#uuid`, with explicit refusal classes including `:non-canonical` and\n`:unpaired-surrogate`. `dao.jing.cbor.md` owns the value-domain contract.\n\n## Phased rollout\n\n### H0: contract and evidence\n\n    New: flat address grammar, algorithm registry descriptors, call-site\n         classification, BLAKE3 fixtures and cross-provider digest table\n    Existing edits: design documentation and conformance resources\n    Must not change: VM H/R or DHT node-id contracts\n\nComplete when:\n\n- `:segment/blake3-<digest>` and `:segment/sha256-<digest>` are pinned as the canonical forms;\n- BLAKE3 and SHA-256 are declared permanent first-class registry entries;\n- BLAKE3 is declared the initial default;\n- official BLAKE3-256 fixtures and representative canonical-value fixtures are recorded;\n- the portability spike's cross-provider digest table is committed;\n- every source use of Jing hash/address operations and every `sha256` literal is classified as frozen contract, new-content mint, existing-address validation, or address-preserving copy (with `request-materialize` and `materialize-async-fn` classified as default-only remote mint entry points, and `store-tree-async` classified as an address-preserving copy flush);\n- the classification records `yin.vm.debruijn/dimension-hash` accurately as a `def`;\n- the absent register implementation is recorded as future integration;\n- malformed, unknown-algorithm, noncanonical-spelling, wrong-length, uppercase, encoder-refusal (returning false for `segment-matches?`), and EDN round-trip cases are specified;\n- an architectural lint/test is specified to reject default `dao.jing/content-hash` or `dao.jing/segment-key` in equality-based validation positions outside `dao.jing`, subject only to narrow reviewed mint assertions; and\n- `docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md` is updated in H0 to replace its SHA-only address examples and pipeline diagram text with multihash addresses.\n\n### H1: registry and whole-system cutover\n\n    Dependencies: the three pinned BLAKE3 providers\n    Existing edits: dao.jing, dependency manifests, backends, DaoSpace,\n                    yin.vm consumers, fixtures, documentation and tests\n    Minting default: BLAKE3\n    Explicit peer: SHA-256\n    Must not change: VM H/R, de Bruijn format hashes, DHT node IDs\n\nComplete when:\n\n- `digest-bytes` and `digest-string` support both algorithms on all three hosts;\n- one-argument `content-hash`, `segment-key`, and `materialize!` use BLAKE3;\n- explicit `{:algorithm :sha256}` minting works across public minting APIs (with `request-materialize` and `materialize-async-fn` documented as default-only remote helpers);\n- parser and accessor tests pass for both canonical forms;\n- all validation sites in the complete audit use `segment-matches?`;\n- all four address-preserving copy sites derive their minting algorithm from the source address (including `store-tree-async` flushes supplying the source address to the remote store);\n- file codec round-trip validation uses the selected algorithm on both sides;\n- `dao.space.index` emits only `:schema-address` and restore accepts only that shape;\n- `yin.vm/primitive-profile` computes its digest explicitly with BLAKE3, keeping its `blake3-` label truthful;\n- every committed development fixture containing segment addresses is regenerated;\n- existing development file stores are discarded rather than upgraded;\n- SHA-only storage docstrings and errors are generalized;\n- the CBOR design's future addressing text alignment completed in H0 is verified active;\n- the architectural lint/test preventing default-following verification is active;\n- CLJ, optimized CLJS, and CLJD builds resolve and execute their providers; and\n- the full repository test suite passes under the new address contract.\n\n### H2: multi-algorithm verification and hardening\n\n    Existing edits: integration tests, CI, provider notes and final docs\n    Must not change: registry identifiers or canonical address spellings\n\nComplete when:\n\n- the same payload can be intentionally materialized and retrieved under both algorithms;\n- each address verifies only under the algorithm it carries;\n- malformed and algorithm-mismatched payloads are rejected;\n- memory, file replay, remote, stepped remote, DHT, ordinary B-tree reads, synchronous hydration, asynchronous hydration, DaoSpace manifests, `yin.vm.content`, `yin.vm.macro`, `yin.vm.semantic`, `yin.vm.completion`, `yin.vm.ledger`, and semantic-bytecode row validation operate correctly with both algorithm selections;\n- DHT caching, both hydration paths, and `store-tree-async` flushes preserve an explicitly SHA-256 address rather than reminting it under BLAKE3;\n- a store can contain both address forms through ordinary post-registry use;\n- remote and DHT peers exchange and validate both forms;\n- official vectors cover empty input, non-ASCII UTF-8, 1023/1024/1025-byte boundaries, and multi-chunk input;\n- randomized cross-host corpora compare complete addresses;\n- optimized CLJS and release CLJD builds are exercised;\n- dependency versions and licenses are recorded;\n- errors name the relevant algorithm without embedding whole sensitive payloads;\n- addresses minted under explicit `{:algorithm :sha256}` continue to verify correctly via `segment-matches?` without depending on or altering `default-hash-algorithm`;\n- `dao.jing.md` distinguishes algorithm, digest, address, minting, verification, and copying;\n- `dao.jing.md` states: \"Verification must be address-directed; minting primitives are not validators\";\n- `dao.jing.md` states that copying addressed content preserves the address-carried algorithm;\n- yin.vm documentation states that H/R remain SHA-256 because of VM contract freeze, not compatibility policy or DaoJing's default;\n- the call-site classification and lint rule are documented as maintenance obligations; and\n- source docstrings agree with the implemented grammar and defaults.\n\n## Test obligations\n\nThe minimum acceptance matrix is:\n\n- SHA-256 and BLAKE3 known-answer tests on CLJ, CLJS, and CLJD.\n- Identical `digest-string` behavior for non-ASCII UTF-8.\n- Identical current canonical bytes and complete addresses across hosts.\n- Committed official-vector and cross-provider fixtures.\n- EDN print/read round-trip for both canonical address forms.\n- Strict rejection of invalid namespace, unknown algorithm, case, length, characters, and noncanonical spelling.\n- Default BLAKE3 and explicit SHA-256 minting.\n- The same payload stored under both algorithms as an ordinary supported operation.\n- Retrieval and verification of both algorithms from one store.\n- Address verification unaffected by minting defaults.\n- Backend collision and `:present` paths using the address-carried algorithm.\n- File codec round-trip checking under the selected algorithm.\n- File replay of a store created under the registry contract containing both algorithms.\n- Remote and DHT exchange of both algorithms.\n- DHT caching of SHA-256 content under its SHA-256 address while BLAKE3 remains the default.\n- Synchronous hydration, asynchronous hydration, and `store-tree-async` flushing preserving the fetched or flushed address's algorithm.\n- DaoSpace candidates containing only `:schema-address`.\n- Direct coverage or an integration path for every validation and copy site in the committed classification.\n- A source-level guard against reintroducing default-following verification.\n- Unchanged stack H and de Bruijn format-hash goldens.\n- Unchanged R goldens when register code lands.\n\nNo test is required solely to demonstrate migration from a pre-registry store, address graph, fixture, or checkpoint shape. CBOR-address fixtures and dual-encoder dispatch tests belong to the separate CBOR clean-break epic.\n\n## Explicit non-goals\n\nThis plan does not:\n\n- encode a canonical-format identifier in segment addresses;\n- support simultaneous canonical encoders;\n- preserve segment addresses across a canonical-encoding change;\n- build a migration path for pre-registry stores or addresses;\n- add an old-address alias or dual checkpoint reader;\n- distinguish SHA-256 content by age or provenance;\n- deprecate SHA-256;\n- force callers to use BLAKE3 when they explicitly choose SHA-256;\n- adopt IPFS binary multihash or CID serialization;\n- introduce runtime algorithm registration;\n- negotiate algorithms implicitly between peers;\n- implement or activate canonical CBOR addressing;\n- alter the existing canonical CBOR codec, corpus, or value-domain decisions;\n- change DHT node identities or Kademlia distance rules;\n- change yin.vm H, R, de Bruijn format hashes, or their contract versions;\n- use BLAKE3 keyed mode, derivation mode, or variable-length output;\n- redesign DaoJing's synchronous handle, effect stream, retention, or garbage collection; or\n- treat hashes as signatures or add authenticity or provenance semantics.\n\nThe result is a cleanly introduced, permanently multi-algorithm content-addressing system: BLAKE3 is the ordinary default, SHA-256 is an equal explicit choice, and every address carries exactly the information needed to select its hash algorithm.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
