runner. It moved once more with\n   the rebase onto master 4529b5bd: the profile digest in the manifest\n   uses BLAKE3 since 492ff50b.\"\n  (keyword (str \"segment/blake3-\"\n                \"b9dbaeff80b320070d24ec870f1b525e\"\n                \"4db0c4cd3214d0ba4c3f2f6b56bea427\")))\n\n\n(def ^:private vector-formats\n  [:yin.semantic/code :yin.debruijn.code :yin.debruijn.register])\n\n10:- SHA-256, an equally supported algorithm available through explicit minting.\n12:SHA-256 is not deprecated, transitional, or a compatibility fallback. A caller may intentionally mint SHA-256 content at any time:\n15:(segment-key value {:algorithm :sha256})\n36::segment/sha256-<64 lowercase hex>\n48:- BLAKE3 and SHA-256 are permanent, first-class algorithm-registry members.\n49:- BLAKE3 is the default for implicit minting; SHA-256 minting is explicit and ordinary.\n73: :sha256 {:address-id \"sha256\"\n112:(sha256 s)\n113:(sha256-bytes bytes)\n118:These functions never follow the default. `sha256` remains useful to VM and DHT contracts independently of its membership in the DaoJing address registry.\n134:No algorithm-specific canonicalization is permitted. BLAKE3 and SHA-256 receive identical bytes for the same value.\n152:(content-hash value {:algorithm :sha256})\n179:(segment-key value {:algorithm :sha256})\n244:This dispatch is the core multihash behavior. A store may contain an explicitly minted SHA-256 object and a default-minted BLAKE3 object at the same time, and each verifies under its own algorithm.\n261:(materialize! handle payload {:algorithm :sha256})\n281:The returned address must equal the source address. This is required for ordinary multi-algorithm operation: a SHA-256 object fetched from a peer remains a SHA-256 object when cached, even though BLAKE3 is the default for unrelated new content.\n316:This makes explicit SHA-256 materialization and default BLAKE3 materialization obey the same file-codec rule.\n325:- Explicit SHA-256 minting is available immediately as an ordinary registry operation.\n332:The absence of migration machinery does not weaken multi-algorithm support. SHA-256 and BLAKE3 coexist because callers may intentionally select either after the registry lands, not because content from an earlier system is being carried forward.\n345:### Direct `jing/sha256` consumers\n363:The DHT node ID remains SHA-256 because it defines the DHT routing\n422:Generation-only calls to `segment-key` remain one-argument calls when BLAKE3 is intended. Callers intentionally choosing SHA-256 pass `{:algorithm :sha256}`.\n485:SHA-256 keeps its current per-host implementations, including the hand-written ClojureDart implementation. That code serves the first-class SHA-256 registry entry and the explicit DHT contract.\n513:- permanent BLAKE3 and SHA-256 entries;\n515:- explicit SHA-256 minting;\n526:- SHA-256 remains explicitly selectable;\n554:- `:segment/blake3-<digest>` and `:segment/sha256-<digest>` are pinned as the canonical forms;\n555:- BLAKE3 and SHA-256 are declared permanent first-class registry entries;\n559:- every source use of Jing hash/address operations and every `sha256` literal is classified as frozen contract, new-content mint, existing-address validation, or address-preserving copy (with `request-materialize` and `materialize-async-fn` classified as default-only remote mint entry points, and `store-tree-async` classified as an address-preserving copy flush);\n572:    Explicit peer: SHA-256\n579:- explicit `{:algorithm :sha256}` minting works across public minting APIs (with `request-materialize` and `materialize-async-fn` documented as default-only remote helpers);\n605:- DHT caching, both hydration paths, and `store-tree-async` flushes preserve an explicitly SHA-256 address rather than reminting it under BLAKE3;\n613:- addresses minted under explicit `{:algorithm :sha256}` continue to verify correctly via `segment-matches?` without depending on or altering `default-hash-algorithm`;\n625:- SHA-256 and BLAKE3 known-answer tests on CLJ, CLJS, and CLJD.\n631:- Default BLAKE3 and explicit SHA-256 minting.\n639:- DHT caching of SHA-256 content under its SHA-256 address while BLAKE3 remains the default.\n657:- distinguish SHA-256 content by age or provenance;\n658:- deprecate SHA-256;\n659:- force callers to use BLAKE3 when they explicitly choose SHA-256;\n671:The result is a cleanly introduced, permanently multi-algorithm content-addressing system: BLAKE3 is the ordinary default, SHA-256 is an equal explicit choice, and every address carries exactly the information needed to select its hash algorithm.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_39","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport re\np=Path('collab/1792620000000-8ab-regen.clj');m='\\\\n'.join(l for l in p.read_text().splitlines() if l.startswith('(prn [:py'));p.write_text(Path('target/8ab/regen-base.clj').read_text()+'\\\\n'+m+'\\\\n')\nfor name,label in [('linked_prelude_test','py'),('linked_safepoint_test','pysp')]:\n p=Path('test/yang/python/antlr/'+name+'.cljc');s=p.read_text();digest=re.search(r'\\\\[:'+label+r' :segment/blake3-([0-9a-f]{64})\\\\]',Path('target/8ab/manifests.log').read_text())[1]\n a=s.index('(def \"'^:private '\"'+('manifest-golden' if label=='py' else 'pysp-manifest-golden'));b=s.index('\\\\n\\\\n',a)\n part=s[a:b];part=re.sub(r'\\\"[0-9a-f]{32}\\\"\\\\s+\\\"[0-9a-f]{32}\\\"','\\\"'+digest+'\\\"',part);s=s[:a]+part+s[b:];p.write_text(s)\nPY\nclojure -M:test -i :slow -n yin.vm.debruijn-test -n yin.vm.debruijn-code-test -n yin.vm.debruijn-register-code-test -n yin.vm.debruijn-register-compile-test -n yin.vm.debruijn.stack-parity-test -n yin.vm.linker-test -n yin.vm.semantic-register.derive-test -n yang.python.antlr.linked-prelude-test -n yang.python.antlr.linked-safepoint-test > target/8ab/touched-slow.log 2>&1\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
