{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_42","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '343,365p' docs/design/dao.jing.hash-registry.md; git diff src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc; sed -n '256,261p' docs/design/dao.jing.call-site-classification.md; rg -n 'slow' test/yang/python/antlr/{linked_prelude_test,linked_safepoint_test}.cljc; tail -8 target/8ab/touched-slow.log; wc -c collab/1792620000000-8ab-regen.clj\"","aggregated_output":"The classification is a maintained design artifact. Minting and verification must not be conflated.\n\n### Direct `jing/sha256` consumers\n\nThe current tree contains five direct consumers:\n\n- `yin.vm.debruijn_code/descriptor-hash`\n- `yin.vm.debruijn_code/image-hash`\n- `yin.vm.debruijn/dimension-hash`, a `def` computed once at namespace load\n- `yin.vm.debruijn/node-hash`\n- `dao.jing.dht/node-id`\n\nThese calls do not follow DaoJing's default.\n\nThe six surviving de Bruijn hash sites use explicit BLAKE3 after the\nphase-8ab regeneration (2026-10-14): stack and register descriptor hashes,\nH and R checksums, and the projection dimension and node hashes. Their\nencoders remain independent of DaoJing's addressing encoder and minting\ndefault. A is the sole code identity; H and R are derived checksums.\n\nThe DHT node ID remains SHA-256 because it defines the DHT routing\nidentity space. Changing it is a separate DHT protocol decision.\n\ndiff --git a/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc b/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc\nindex 762202ac..576d6672 100644\n--- a/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc\n+++ b/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_code.cljc\n@@ -117,12 +117,7 @@\n (def descriptor\n   \"The `:yin.debruijn.code/*` dimension declared as data (S2): a lowering-\n    contract version, arity (the opcode table's own mnemonic count), the\n-   ordered opcode table, the canonical encoding rule, and one lift\n-   morphism from `:yin.debruijn.code/*` to `:yin.code/*` -- declared and\n-   exported here as a documented contract point only. The morphism's\n-   exact shape and implementation are B2's file box; this phase names\n-   only its target (`:yin.code/*`) here as data; its intended rule\n-   (diagnostic binder names or synthesized names for bound positions) is\n+   ordered opcode table and the canonical encoding rule. The H formula is\n    prose, kept in this docstring rather than hashed, per P3-4 of the\n    debruijn-b1-fix review: this whole vector feeds `descriptor-hash` (via\n    `encode-scalar`), so an editorial change to a hashed sentence would\n@@ -135,14 +130,13 @@\n    [:yin.debruijn.code/dimension :dim/slots opcode-table]\n    [:yin.debruijn.code/dimension\n     :dim/encoding\n-    {:hash :sha256,\n+    {:hash :blake3,\n      :scalar-classes scalar-classes,\n      :framing :tagged-and-length-delimited,\n      :fixed-width-kinds [:uint :pc],\n      :nfc false,\n      :integral-double-folding false,\n-     :map-set-order :canonical-encoded-bytes}]\n-   [:yin.debruijn.code/dimension :dim/lift-to [:yin.code/*]]])\n+     :map-set-order :canonical-encoded-bytes}]])\n \n \n ;; =============================================================================\n@@ -545,23 +539,23 @@\n    encoder' shape `yin.vm.debruijn/dimension-hash` uses for its\n    descriptor, computed here with this dimension's distinct encoder\n    rather than that one.\"\n-  (jing/sha256 (encode-scalar descriptor)))\n+  (jing/blake3 (encode-scalar descriptor)))\n \n \n (defn image-hash\n-  \"The ONE function that computes H (S2 item 6): `jing/sha256` over the\n+  \"The ONE function that computes H (S2 item 6): `jing/blake3` over the\n    descriptor hash (which already folds in the lowering-contract\n    version) concatenated with the canonical instruction vector's own\n    bytes -- pc-indexed tuples, refs already resolved to pcs, no header,\n-   exact scalar bytes. `jing/sha256`, not `jing/sha256-bytes`: this\n+   exact scalar bytes. `jing/blake3`, not `jing/blake3-bytes`: this\n    dimension's canonical encoding is hex text (`encode-image` returns a\n-   string, via `framed`/`to-hex`), the same shape `jing/sha256` hashes\n+   string, via `framed`/`to-hex`), the same shape `jing/blake3` hashes\n    for `yin.vm.debruijn/dimension-hash`, so hashing the string directly\n-   is the fit; `sha256-bytes` would apply to a raw byte buffer this\n-   encoder never produces. H is the ONLY executable and sharing identity\n-   this file computes: no projection fingerprint, no second identity.\"\n+   is the fit; `blake3-bytes` would apply to a raw byte buffer this\n+   encoder never produces. H is a derived checksum of this backend image;\n+   A is the sole code identity.\"\n   [v]\n-  (jing/sha256 (str descriptor-hash (encode-image v))))\n+  (jing/blake3 (str descriptor-hash (encode-image v))))\n \n \n ;; =============================================================================\ndiff --git a/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc b/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc\nindex c9039fae..a7039097 100644\n--- a/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc\n+++ b/src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_code.cljc\n@@ -50,8 +50,9 @@\n ;; and sparse continuation formats.\n \n (def contract-version\n-  \"Bumped whenever this dimension's opcode table, allocation, scalar\n-   framing, or control-flow rules change shape (design section 3).\n+  \"The descriptor records this dimension's opcode table, allocation, scalar\n+   framing, and control-flow rules (design section 3). Phase 8 regenerates\n+   descriptors and checksums without a version bump (owner ruling 2).\n    3, as of design sections 4.4-4.6 and 5.2: the complete R2 register\n    effects set, in-band live sets on all six boundary opcodes, and\n    sparse continuation integration. 4 (the `\\\"r2\\\"` contract,\n@@ -191,17 +192,16 @@\n   \"The `:yin.debruijn.register/*` dimension declared as data (design\n    section 3): a contract version, arity, the opcode table, the encoding\n    rule (which names B1's scalar domain by reference, not by copy: this\n-   dimension has no scalar table of its own), and the lift target.\"\n+   dimension has no scalar table of its own).\"\n   [[:yin.debruijn.register/dimension :dim/contract-version contract-version]\n    [:yin.debruijn.register/dimension :dim/arity (count opcode-table)]\n    [:yin.debruijn.register/dimension :dim/slots opcode-table]\n    [:yin.debruijn.register/dimension\n     :dim/encoding\n-    {:hash :sha256,\n+    {:hash :blake3,\n      :scalar-encoder :yin.debruijn-code/encode-scalar,\n      :register-banks [:locals :temporaries],\n-     :map-set-order :canonical-encoded-bytes}]\n-   [:yin.debruijn.register/dimension :dim/lift-to [:yin.code/*]]])\n+     :map-set-order :canonical-encoded-bytes}]])\n \n \n ;; =============================================================================\n@@ -255,18 +255,18 @@\n \n \n (def descriptor-hash\n-  (jing/sha256 (debruijn-code/encode-scalar descriptor)))\n+  (jing/blake3 (debruijn-code/encode-scalar descriptor)))\n \n \n (defn register-hash\n-  \"The ONE function that computes R (design section 3): `R = sha256\n+  \"The ONE function that computes R (design section 3): `R = blake3\n    (register-descriptor-hash || canonical-register-vector)`. Received\n    register bytes must be hashed before decoding, as B1's wire rule\n    requires -- no receive path exists in this phase, but this function's\n    own shape (bytes in, hash out, no host-value peeking) already supports\n    that later use.\"\n   [register-image]\n-  (jing/sha256 (str descriptor-hash (encode-register-image register-image))))\n+  (jing/blake3 (str descriptor-hash (encode-register-image register-image))))\n \n \n ;; =============================================================================\n- **14** direct Class-2 mint call sites, **2** default-only remote mint\n  entry points, and **1** DaoSpace mint site requiring an H1 field-shape\n  change (`:schema-hash` → `:schema-address`) — 17 rows total.\n- **20** Class-3 address-directed validation call sites (covering the\n  design's 18 enumerated bullets), all currently using one of two\n  non-conforming comparison shapes and all requiring conversion to\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_safepoint_test.cljc:15:   or links is slow.\"\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_safepoint_test.cljc:17:            [dao.test-slow :as slow]\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_safepoint_test.cljc:265:(deftest ^:slow pysp-manifest-address-test\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_safepoint_test.cljc:266:  (slow/guard \"pysp-manifest-address-test\" pysp-manifest-address))\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_safepoint_test.cljc:342:(deftest ^:slow linked-fail-closed-test\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_safepoint_test.cljc:343:  (slow/guard \"linked-fail-closed-test\" fail-closed))\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_safepoint_test.cljc:358:(deftest ^:slow linked-interrupt-test\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_safepoint_test.cljc:359:  (slow/guard \"linked-interrupt-test\" interrupt))\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_safepoint_test.cljc:370:(deftest ^:slow linked-no-park-test\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_safepoint_test.cljc:371:  (slow/guard \"linked-no-park-test\" no-park))\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_safepoint_test.cljc:389:(deftest ^:slow linked-registered-handler-test\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_safepoint_test.cljc:390:  (slow/guard \"linked-registered-handler-test\" registered-handler-runs))\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_safepoint_test.cljc:402:(deftest ^:slow linked-recursion-error-test\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_safepoint_test.cljc:403:  (slow/guard \"linked-recursion-error-test\" recursion-error))\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_safepoint_test.cljc:416:(deftest ^:slow linked-escape-restores-depth-test\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_safepoint_test.cljc:417:  (slow/guard \"linked-escape-restores-depth-test\" escape-restores-depth))\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_safepoint_test.cljc:432:(deftest ^:slow linked-generator-depth-test\ntest/yang/python/antl